window.__RFR02_DATA = {"content": {"meta": {"title": "Regulations for Registration (RfR)", "edition": "Fifth edition · Working draft 7", "banner": "Prototype. Presents Working Draft 7 of RfR v5 in a digital-first format. Not approved, not an Engineering Council position, and not an authoritative version of the Regulations.", "source": "RfR_v5_Working_Draft_7.md", "generated": "2026-10-15", "sections": 11, "clauses": 83, "licences": {"core": "Applies to every Licensee", "registration": "Registration licence (held by every Licensee)", "recognition": "Licence to recognise programmes of learning", "ipd": "Licence to accredit IPD schemes"}}, "frontmatter": [{"title": "Hierarchy of Regulations and Standards", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-1-01", "html": "<p>The Engineering Council is the UK’s regulatory body for the engineering profession. It operates under a Royal Charter and is governed by a Board that represents UK Licensees as well as individuals from industries and sectors with an interest in regulation of the profession.</p>", "text": "The Engineering Council is the UK’s regulatory body for the engineering profession. It operates under a Royal Charter and is governed by a Board that represents UK Licensees as well as individuals from industries and sectors with an interest in regulation of the profession.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-1-02", "html": "<p>This document is one in a series of closely related publications:</p><ul><li>Regulations for Registration (RfR)</li><li>Regulations for Licensing (RfL)</li><li>The UK Standard for Professional Engineering Competence and Commitment (UK-SPEC)</li><li>Information and Communications Technology Technician Standard (the ICT<em>Tech</em> Standard)</li><li>Approval and Accreditation of Qualifications and Apprenticeships (AAQA)</li><li>Accreditation of Higher Education Programmes (AHEP)</li></ul>", "text": "This document is one in a series of closely related publications: Regulations for Registration (RfR) Regulations for Licensing (RfL) The UK Standard for Professional Engineering Competence and Commitment (UK-SPEC) Information and Communications Technology Technician Standard (the ICT Tech Standard) Approval and Accreditation of Qualifications and Apprenticeships (AAQA) Accreditation of Higher Education Programmes (AHEP)", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-1-03", "html": "<p>The Engineering Council publishes these documents on behalf of the UK engineering profession, with whom they were developed and are kept under review. The Engineering Council also publishes policy statements, regulations explanatory notes for institutions, and guidance for individuals.</p>", "text": "The Engineering Council publishes these documents on behalf of the UK engineering profession, with whom they were developed and are kept under review. The Engineering Council also publishes policy statements, regulations explanatory notes for institutions, and guidance for individuals.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}], "subsections": [], "id": "FM-1", "anchor": "hierarchy-of-regulations-and-standards"}, {"title": "Welcome", "blocks": [], "subsections": [{"title": "The purpose of the Regulations for Registration", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-01", "html": "<p>This document is the Regulations for Registration (RfR).</p>", "text": "This document is the Regulations for Registration (RfR).", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-02", "html": "<p>The primary purpose of RfR is to set out the Regulations that Licensees of the Engineering Council must adhere to when registering applicants, recognising programmes of learning and development, and undertaking related processes.</p>", "text": "The primary purpose of RfR is to set out the Regulations that Licensees of the Engineering Council must adhere to when registering applicants, recognising programmes of learning and development, and undertaking related processes.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}], "anchor": "the-purpose-of-the-regulations-for-registration"}, {"title": "Who the Regulations for Registration are for", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-03", "html": "<p>Licensees use this document to ensure their processes meet the requirements of their licence.</p>", "text": "Licensees use this document to ensure their processes meet the requirements of their licence.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-04", "html": "<p>The Engineering Council’s Quality Assurance Committee and its licensing function use this document to support the regular licence reviews of Licensees.</p>", "text": "The Engineering Council’s Quality Assurance Committee and its licensing function use this document to support the regular licence reviews of Licensees.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}], "anchor": "who-the-regulations-for-registration-are-for"}, {"title": "Licensee", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-05", "html": "<p>Throughout the Regulations, ‘Licensee’ means an organisation licensed by the Engineering Council Board to assess individuals for professional registration. To become a Licensee, an organisation must demonstrate to the satisfaction of the Engineering Council Board that it is competent to perform this function and regulate the conduct of its members.</p>", "text": "Throughout the Regulations, ‘Licensee’ means an organisation licensed by the Engineering Council Board to assess individuals for professional registration. To become a Licensee, an organisation must demonstrate to the satisfaction of the Engineering Council Board that it is competent to perform this function and regulate the conduct of its members.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-06", "html": "<p>Additionally, Licensees can be licensed to recognise programmes of learning and competence development to specific standards.</p>", "text": "Additionally, Licensees can be licensed to recognise programmes of learning and competence development to specific standards.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "id": "FM-2-07", "html": "<p>Licensees are sometimes known informally as Professional Engineering Institutions, or PEIs.</p>", "text": "Licensees are sometimes known informally as Professional Engineering Institutions, or PEIs.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}], "anchor": "licensee"}], "id": "FM-2", "anchor": "welcome"}], "sections": [{"title": "Definitions and peer review", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "EX-01-01", "html": "<p>These definitions apply throughout these Regulations.</p>", "text": "These definitions apply throughout these Regulations.", "refs": [], "status": "new"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-001", "kind": "definition", "term": "peer", "v44": "v4.4 paras 22, 25, 31, 41-43, 61", "v44Paras": [22, 25, 31, 41, 42, 43, 61], "num": "1.1", "html": "<p>A <em>peer</em> is a professionally registered individual whose experience and training enable them to make an informed professional judgment about the professional review, programme recognition, or other matters.</p>", "text": "A peer is a professionally registered individual whose experience and training enable them to make an informed professional judgment about the professional review, programme recognition, or other matters.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-002", "kind": "definition", "term": "peer review", "v44": "v4.4 paras 22-25, 31, 41-43, 61", "v44Paras": [22, 23, 24, 25, 31, 41, 42, 43, 61], "num": "1.2", "html": "<p>A <em>peer review</em> is an assessment in which one or more peers consider appropriate evidence against defined requirements and make a documented professional judgment.</p>", "text": "A peer review is an assessment in which one or more peers consider appropriate evidence against defined requirements and make a documented professional judgment.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-074", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 22 (new clause built from it)", "num": "1.3", "html": "<p>Where these Regulations require peer review, Licensees must ensure that:</p><ul><li>each peer is suitably qualified and trained for the role;</li><li>at least one peer has appropriate and relevant engineering experience, sufficient to make an informed judgment on the matter being assessed; and</li><li>all reasonable steps are taken in the selection of peers to ensure that potential conflicts of interest are avoided.</li></ul>", "text": "Where these Regulations require peer review, Licensees must ensure that: each peer is suitably qualified and trained for the role; at least one peer has appropriate and relevant engineering experience, sufficient to make an informed judgment on the matter being assessed; and all reasonable steps are taken in the selection of peers to ensure that potential conflicts of interest are avoided.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "v44Paras": [22], "tables": ["T3"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-003", "kind": "definition", "term": "committee responsible for registration", "v44": "v4.4 paras 17, 25, 27", "v44Paras": [17, 25, 27], "num": "1.4", "html": "<p>The <em>committee responsible for registration</em> is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its registration activity and make or authorise decisions on applications for registration.</p>", "text": "The committee responsible for registration is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its registration activity and make or authorise decisions on applications for registration.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-004", "kind": "definition", "term": "committee responsible for recognition", "v44": "v4.4 paras 41-44, 47-55, 63", "v44Paras": [41, 42, 43, 44, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 63], "num": "1.5", "html": "<p>A <em>committee responsible for recognition</em> is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its programme-recognition activity and its accreditation of IPD schemes, and to make or authorise decisions on the recognition of programmes and the accreditation of schemes. This may be one and the same as the committee responsible for registration.</p>", "text": "A committee responsible for recognition is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its programme-recognition activity and its accreditation of IPD schemes, and to make or authorise decisions on the recognition of programmes and the accreditation of schemes. This may be one and the same as the committee responsible for registration.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {"may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-075", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 25 (third sentence) and para 41 (second sentence)", "v44Paras": [25, 41], "num": "1.6", "html": "<p>Engineering Council registrants must be in the majority on the committee responsible for registration and on the committee responsible for recognition.</p>", "text": "Engineering Council registrants must be in the majority on the committee responsible for registration and on the committee responsible for recognition.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-005", "kind": "definition", "term": "recognition", "v44": "v4.4 paras 37, 38, 40, 62 (definitional parts)", "v44Paras": [37, 38, 40, 62], "num": "1.7", "html": "<p><em>Recognition</em> is the umbrella term for approval and accreditation.</p>", "text": "Recognition is the umbrella term for approval and accreditation.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-006", "kind": "definition", "term": "approval", "v44": "v4.4 paras 37, 38, 40, 62 (definitional parts)", "v44Paras": [37, 38, 40, 62], "num": "1.8", "html": "<p>Approval of a programme of learning attests to its content: its overall design, depth and range of coverage, and the validity and reliability of the assessment of the learner.</p>", "text": "Approval of a programme of learning attests to its content: its overall design, depth and range of coverage, and the validity and reliability of the assessment of the learner.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-007", "kind": "definition", "term": "accreditation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 37, 38, 40, 62 (definitional parts)", "v44Paras": [37, 38, 40, 62], "num": "1.9", "html": "<p>Accreditation confirms that the delivery of a programme of learning, or of an IPD scheme, is recognised at a specific site or sites, or at a specific provider. Accreditation incorporates approval: if delivery is accredited, approval of the underlying content is implicit.</p>", "text": "Accreditation confirms that the delivery of a programme of learning, or of an IPD scheme, is recognised at a specific site or sites, or at a specific provider. Accreditation incorporates approval: if delivery is accredited, approval of the underlying content is implicit.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-008", "kind": "definition", "term": "joint board", "v44": "v4.4 paras 42, 63", "v44Paras": [42, 63], "num": "1.10", "html": "<p>A <em>joint board</em> is a single decision-making body established by two or more Licensees to recognise programmes on their behalf, such as the Joint Board of Moderators.</p>", "text": "A joint board is a single decision-making body established by two or more Licensees to recognise programmes on their behalf, such as the Joint Board of Moderators.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-009", "kind": "definition", "term": "joint visit", "v44": "v4.4 paras 42, 63", "v44Paras": [42, 63], "num": "1.11", "html": "<p>A <em>joint visit</em> is an accreditation visit carried out by two or more Licensees together, each of which makes its own recognition decision.</p>", "text": "A joint visit is an accreditation visit carried out by two or more Licensees together, each of which makes its own recognition decision.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-010", "kind": "definition", "term": "interim registrant", "v44": "v4.4 para 15 (interim) and para 10's '(i.e. not interim)' gloss (final stage)", "v44Paras": [10, 15], "num": "1.12", "html": "<p>An interim registrant is a person whose underpinning knowledge and understanding have been assessed as meeting the Standard, but who has not yet demonstrated the necessary competence for final-stage registration.</p>", "text": "An interim registrant is a person whose underpinning knowledge and understanding have been assessed as meeting the Standard, but who has not yet demonstrated the necessary competence for final-stage registration.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "DEF-011", "kind": "definition", "term": "final-stage registrant", "v44": "v4.4 para 15 (interim) and para 10's '(i.e. not interim)' gloss (final stage)", "v44Paras": [10, 15], "num": "1.13", "html": "<p>A final-stage registrant is a person who has successfully completed the Professional Review and holds registered status that is not interim.</p>", "text": "A final-stage registrant is a person who has successfully completed the Professional Review and holds registered status that is not interim.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [], "num": 1, "id": "S01", "anchor": "definitions-and-peer-review", "licence": "core"}, {"title": "Introduction and governance", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 1", "v44Paras": [1], "id": "EX-02-01", "html": "<p>These Regulations for Registration are published by the Board of the Engineering Council and govern the implementation by Licensees of the UK Standard for Professional Engineering Competence and Commitment (UK-SPEC) and related Recognised Standards, and the registration of individuals under those Standards.</p>", "text": "These Regulations for Registration are published by the Board of the Engineering Council and govern the implementation by Licensees of the UK Standard for Professional Engineering Competence and Commitment (UK-SPEC) and related Recognised Standards, and the registration of individuals under those Standards.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 2", "v44Paras": [2], "id": "EX-02-02", "html": "<p>Nothing in these Regulations overrides obligations placed on the Engineering Council and Licensees by national and international legislation, including any requirement to process applicants holding recognised international qualifications or credentials.</p>", "text": "Nothing in these Regulations overrides obligations placed on the Engineering Council and Licensees by national and international legislation, including any requirement to process applicants holding recognised international qualifications or credentials.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 4", "v44Paras": [4], "id": "EX-02-03", "html": "<p>The Board of the Engineering Council may add to, amend, or revoke paragraphs in these Regulations, on the recommendation of the Registration Standards Committee, or may delegate responsibility for amendment to that committee. Any such changes will be communicated to all Licensees.</p>", "text": "The Board of the Engineering Council may add to, amend, or revoke paragraphs in these Regulations, on the recommendation of the Registration Standards Committee, or may delegate responsibility for amendment to that committee. Any such changes will be communicated to all Licensees.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 7", "v44Paras": [7], "id": "EX-02-04", "html": "<p>The Engineering Council publishes guidance on the professional review, recognition of learning, and other areas covered by these Regulations. The standing of each type of Engineering Council publication is set out in the Code of Practice on Document Hierarchy and Associated Language.</p>", "text": "The Engineering Council publishes guidance on the professional review, recognition of learning, and other areas covered by these Regulations. The standing of each type of Engineering Council publication is set out in the Code of Practice on Document Hierarchy and Associated Language.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-003", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 3 and 6", "v44Paras": [3, 6], "num": "2.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must submit their application of these Regulations to audit by the Engineering Council’s Quality Assurance Committee (QAC), including the licence reviews set out in the Regulations for Licensing. Licensees must maintain sufficient records for external audit purposes, and must have a suitable management system and self-assessment process in place, in order to demonstrate and maintain conformity with the Engineering Council requirements.</p>", "text": "Licensees must submit their application of these Regulations to audit by the Engineering Council’s Quality Assurance Committee (QAC), including the licence reviews set out in the Regulations for Licensing. Licensees must maintain sufficient records for external audit purposes, and must have a suitable management system and self-assessment process in place, in order to demonstrate and maintain conformity with the Engineering Council requirements.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-005", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 5", "v44Paras": [5], "num": "2.2", "html": "<p>Licensees must not register individuals with the Engineering Council, or recognise programmes of development and learning for such registration, outside the terms of these Regulations for Registration, without the authorisation of the Registration Standards Committee.</p>", "text": "Licensees must not register individuals with the Engineering Council, or recognise programmes of development and learning for such registration, outside the terms of these Regulations for Registration, without the authorisation of the Registration Standards Committee.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must not": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-007", "kind": "regulation", "num": "2.3", "html": "<p>Licensees must follow the Engineering Council’s Regulations Explanatory Notes and Policy Statements. Licensees should follow the Engineering Council's Guidance Notes relating to these Regulations. Licensees must refer technical or procedural issues arising in the application of these Regulations to the Engineering Council for consideration, following which the Engineering Council may issue further guidance.</p>", "text": "Licensees must follow the Engineering Council’s Regulations Explanatory Notes and Policy Statements. Licensees should follow the Engineering Council's Guidance Notes relating to these Regulations. Licensees must refer technical or procedural issues arising in the application of these Regulations to the Engineering Council for consideration, following which the Engineering Council may issue further guidance.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "should": 1, "may": 1}, "v44": "v4.4 para 7 (second sentence)", "v44Paras": [7], "tables": []}], "subsections": [], "num": 2, "id": "S02", "anchor": "introduction-and-governance", "licence": "core"}, {"title": "Professional registration and the Professional Review", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 11", "v44Paras": [11], "id": "EX-03-01", "html": "<p>Competence and commitment will be developed through a combination of:</p><ul><li>underpinning knowledge and understanding;</li><li>professional development and experience.</li></ul>", "text": "Competence and commitment will be developed through a combination of: underpinning knowledge and understanding; professional development and experience.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 11", "v44Paras": [11], "id": "EX-03-02", "html": "<p>Licensees assess an applicant’s underpinning knowledge and understanding before, or alongside, the Professional Review of their competence and commitment. An applicant cannot be awarded final-stage registration until the required underpinning knowledge and understanding have been demonstrated (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-001\" data-ref=\"UKU-001\">UKU-001</a>). The stages of assessment, and the gate between each, are summarised in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#T1\" data-ref=\"T1\">Table 1</a>.</p>", "text": "Licensees assess an applicant’s underpinning knowledge and understanding before, or alongside, the Professional Review of their competence and commitment. An applicant cannot be awarded final-stage registration until the required underpinning knowledge and understanding have been demonstrated (UKU-001). The stages of assessment, and the gate between each, are summarised in Table 1.", "refs": ["T1", "UKU-001"], "status": "new"}, {"type": "table", "caption": "Table 1: stages of assessment", "header": ["Stage", "What happens", "Who", "Gate before the next stage", "Clauses"], "rows": [["Initial assessment", "Clerical check that the application is complete and the evidence is genuine", "Licensee staff, under the committee responsible for registration", "Application complete and verified before any peer review", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-017\" data-ref=\"V5-017\">V5-017</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-018\" data-ref=\"V5-018\">V5-018</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-076\" data-ref=\"V5-076\">V5-076</a>"], ["Assessment of underpinning knowledge and understanding", "Determination that UKU meets the requirement, and any gap identified and addressed", "Peers, drawing on verified evidence; supported by staff for clerical checks", "UKU met before final-stage registration. Interim registration is possible from this point", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-001\" data-ref=\"UKU-001\">UKU-001</a> to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-018\" data-ref=\"UKU-018\">UKU-018</a>"], ["Professional Review: review of documentary evidence", "First peer assessment of competence and commitment", "Two peers", "Recommendation from the assessors of the documentary evidence before interview", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-019\" data-ref=\"V5-019\">V5-019</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-020\" data-ref=\"V5-020\">V5-020</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-022\" data-ref=\"V5-022\">V5-022</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-077\" data-ref=\"V5-077\">V5-077</a>"], ["Professional Review: interview", "Second peer assessment of competence and commitment", "Two peers", "Both assessors recommend registration", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-078\" data-ref=\"V5-078\">V5-078</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-022\" data-ref=\"V5-022\">V5-022</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-024\" data-ref=\"V5-024\">V5-024</a>"], ["Registration decision", "The committee decides whether to register", "Committee responsible for registration", "-", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-025\" data-ref=\"V5-025\">V5-025</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-026\" data-ref=\"V5-026\">V5-026</a>"]], "id": "T1", "rowAppliesTo": [["V5-017", "V5-018", "V5-076"], ["UKU-001", "UKU-003", "UKU-004", "UKU-006", "UKU-007", "UKU-008", "UKU-009", "UKU-011", "UKU-012", "UKU-014", "UKU-016", "UKU-017", "UKU-018"], ["V5-019", "V5-020", "V5-022", "V5-077"], ["V5-022", "V5-024", "V5-078"], ["V5-025", "V5-026"]], "appliesTo": ["UKU-001", "UKU-003", "UKU-004", "UKU-006", "UKU-007", "UKU-008", "UKU-009", "UKU-011", "UKU-012", "UKU-014", "UKU-016", "UKU-017", "UKU-018", "V5-017", "V5-018", "V5-019", "V5-020", "V5-022", "V5-024", "V5-025", "V5-026", "V5-076", "V5-077", "V5-078"]}], "subsections": [{"title": "Standards", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 8", "v44Paras": [8], "id": "EX-03-03", "html": "<p>UK-SPEC and the ICT<em>Tech</em> Standard are Prescribed Standards of the Engineering Council. They set out the competence and commitment required for each category of registration, and are the standards against which Licensees assess applicants.</p>", "text": "UK-SPEC and the ICT Tech Standard are Prescribed Standards of the Engineering Council. They set out the competence and commitment required for each category of registration, and are the standards against which Licensees assess applicants.", "refs": [], "status": "new"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 8", "v44Paras": [8], "id": "EX-03-04", "html": "<p>Where these Regulations refer to UK-SPEC or ‘the Standard’, they refer equally to Recognised Standards derived or developed by Licensees or the Engineering Council, including the Information and Communications Technology Technician (ICT<em>Tech</em>) Standard.</p>", "text": "Where these Regulations refer to UK-SPEC or ‘the Standard’, they refer equally to Recognised Standards derived or developed by Licensees or the Engineering Council, including the Information and Communications Technology Technician (ICT Tech) Standard.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "table", "caption": "Table 2: Recognised Standards", "header": ["Standard", "Developed by", "Basis", "Authorisation"], "rows": [["UK-SPEC; the ICT<em>Tech</em> Standard", "Engineering Council", "Prescribed Standards", "Not applicable"], ["Proprietary Standard", "Engineering Council", "Based on and fully meeting UK-SPEC, such as sector-specific or specialist standards", "Not applicable"], ["Derived Standard", "A Licensee", "Based on and fully meeting UK-SPEC or a Proprietary Standard", "QAC (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-012\" data-ref=\"V5-012\">V5-012</a>)"]], "id": "T2"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 13", "v44Paras": [13], "id": "EX-03-05", "html": "<p>Proprietary Standards and Derived Standards are collectively described as ‘Recognised Standards’. Further detail is set out in the Guidance Note on Recognised Standards.</p>", "text": "Proprietary Standards and Derived Standards are collectively described as ‘Recognised Standards’. Further detail is set out in the Guidance Note on Recognised Standards.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-012", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 12 and 13 (QAC authorisation)", "v44Paras": [12, 13], "num": "3.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must establish procedures for assessing applicants for registration against UK-SPEC. A Licensee may instead adopt its own standard of competence and commitment, derived from and fully meeting UK-SPEC or a Proprietary Standard, which can be assessed objectively and which relates to the technologies and applications with which it is concerned, provided the standard has been authorised by QAC as a Derived Standard.</p>", "text": "Licensees must establish procedures for assessing applicants for registration against UK-SPEC. A Licensee may instead adopt its own standard of competence and commitment, derived from and fully meeting UK-SPEC or a Proprietary Standard, which can be assessed objectively and which relates to the technologies and applications with which it is concerned, provided the standard has been authorised by QAC as a Derived Standard.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-014", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 14", "v44Paras": [14], "num": "3.2", "html": "<p>Recognised Standards may make requirements of the assessment and revalidation of applicants additional to those in UK-SPEC and these Regulations. Licensees’ processes for assessing applicants for registration and revalidation must comply with any such requirements. Individuals holding registration against a Proprietary Standard may transfer to a corresponding Principal Register category without further assessment, subject to meeting any discipline-specific requirements of the registering Licensee.</p>", "text": "Recognised Standards may make requirements of the assessment and revalidation of applicants additional to those in UK-SPEC and these Regulations. Licensees’ processes for assessing applicants for registration and revalidation must comply with any such requirements. Individuals holding registration against a Proprietary Standard may transfer to a corresponding Principal Register category without further assessment, subject to meeting any discipline-specific requirements of the registering Licensee.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 2}, "tables": []}], "id": "S03.1", "anchor": "standards"}, {"title": "Registration", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-009", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 9 and 10", "v44Paras": [9, 10], "num": "3.3", "html": "<p>Licensees must only register persons who have demonstrated the competence and commitment appropriate to a registration category, and only in categories they are licensed to register. An individual may hold final-stage registration in more than one category of the Register only in exceptional circumstances, whether with one Licensee or more. Guidance about the exceptional circumstances is available. Where an individual seeks such registration with the same Licensee, the Licensee must consider the case individually and assess the applicant against each category independently.</p>", "text": "Licensees must only register persons who have demonstrated the competence and commitment appropriate to a registration category, and only in categories they are licensed to register. An individual may hold final-stage registration in more than one category of the Register only in exceptional circumstances, whether with one Licensee or more. Guidance about the exceptional circumstances is available. Where an individual seeks such registration with the same Licensee, the Licensee must consider the case individually and assess the applicant against each category independently.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S03.2", "anchor": "registration"}], "num": 3, "id": "S03", "anchor": "professional-registration-and-the-professional-review", "licence": "registration"}, {"title": "Initial assessment", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 16", "v44Paras": [16], "id": "EX-04-01", "html": "<p>The initial assessment is a clerical check to confirm that an application is complete and that the evidence submitted is genuine. For example, confirming a qualification’s recognised status on the Engineering Council’s recognised programmes database, or verifying certificates. The initial assessment is not peer review; it ensures the application is ready for peer review.</p>", "text": "The initial assessment is a clerical check to confirm that an application is complete and that the evidence submitted is genuine. For example, confirming a qualification’s recognised status on the Engineering Council’s recognised programmes database, or verifying certificates. The initial assessment is not peer review; it ensures the application is ready for peer review.", "refs": [], "status": "new"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-017", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 17", "v44Paras": [17], "num": "4.1", "html": "<p>The committee responsible for registration is responsible for the initial assessment and may delegate it to staff. Any delegation to staff must be on the basis of clear procedures and guidance established by the committee, and must be subject to internal audit under the self-assessment process required by <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-003\" data-ref=\"V5-003\">V5-003</a>.</p>", "text": "The committee responsible for registration is responsible for the initial assessment and may delegate it to staff. Any delegation to staff must be on the basis of clear procedures and guidance established by the committee, and must be subject to internal audit under the self-assessment process required by V5-003.", "refs": ["V5-003"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-018", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 18 and 21", "v44Paras": [18, 21], "num": "4.2", "html": "<p>The Licensee must tell applicants what documentation is required, and in what format. This may include their:</p><ul><li>career history;</li><li>education and training record; and</li><li>any evidence of experiential, or on-the-job, learning.</li></ul><p>The Licensee must take reasonable steps to verify that the evidence submitted is authentic, is the applicant’s own work, and accurately represents their knowledge, experience and achievements.</p><p>Where the Licensee considers that additional evidence of knowledge and understanding, or of competence and commitment, is required, it must tell the applicant what further evidence is needed.</p>", "text": "The Licensee must tell applicants what documentation is required, and in what format. This may include their: career history; education and training record; and any evidence of experiential, or on-the-job, learning. The Licensee must take reasonable steps to verify that the evidence submitted is authentic, is the applicant’s own work, and accurately represents their knowledge, experience and achievements. Where the Licensee considers that additional evidence of knowledge and understanding, or of competence and commitment, is required, it must tell the applicant what further evidence is needed.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-076", "kind": "regulation", "v44": null, "num": "4.3", "html": "<p>Licensees must not refer an application for peer review until the initial assessment has confirmed that the required evidence is complete and has been verified.</p>", "text": "Licensees must not refer an application for peer review until the initial assessment has confirmed that the required evidence is complete and has been verified.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {"must not": 1}, "v44Paras": [], "tables": ["T1"]}], "subsections": [], "num": 4, "id": "S04", "anchor": "initial-assessment", "licence": "registration"}, {"title": "Assessment of underpinning knowledge and understanding", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 28", "v44Paras": [28], "id": "EX-05-01", "html": "<p>Underpinning knowledge and understanding provide the foundation for competent professional practice. Licensees determine whether each applicant has demonstrated the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for the category of registration sought.</p>", "text": "Underpinning knowledge and understanding provide the foundation for competent professional practice. Licensees determine whether each applicant has demonstrated the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for the category of registration sought.", "refs": [], "status": "new"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-016", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 12 ('establish criteria and procedures')", "v44Paras": [12], "num": "5.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must establish procedures for assessing underpinning knowledge and understanding by each of the methods they accept. Where an assessment requires a professional judgment, rather than the verification of a recognised qualification, it must be made by peer review (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>).</p>", "text": "Licensees must establish procedures for assessing underpinning knowledge and understanding by each of the methods they accept. Where an assessment requires a professional judgment, rather than the verification of a recognised qualification, it must be made by peer review (V5-074).", "refs": ["V5-074"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-007", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 18, 28", "v44Paras": [18, 28], "num": "5.2", "html": "<p>The applicable Engineering Council Knowledge Benchmark is the reference against which Licensees must determine whether each applicant’s underpinning knowledge and understanding meet the requirement, including applicants who hold a recognised programme. Where the evidence leaves a gap, Licensees must use the Knowledge Benchmark to identify it.</p>", "text": "The applicable Engineering Council Knowledge Benchmark is the reference against which Licensees must determine whether each applicant’s underpinning knowledge and understanding meet the requirement, including applicants who hold a recognised programme. Where the evidence leaves a gap, Licensees must use the Knowledge Benchmark to identify it.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-003", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 28; paras 29-33", "v44Paras": [28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33], "num": "5.3", "html": "<p>Underpinning knowledge and understanding may be developed through any appropriate method or combination of methods. In making its determination, a Licensee may consider evidence including:</p><ul><li>recognised qualifications or programmes of learning;</li><li>other formal qualifications or programmes of study;</li><li>work-based or experiential learning;</li><li>in-employment education and training;</li><li>self-directed learning;</li><li>technical reports or other technical evidence;</li><li>technical interviews, presentations, examinations or other appropriate assessment methods;</li><li>relevant existing Engineering Council registration; or</li><li>any combination of these.</li></ul>", "text": "Underpinning knowledge and understanding may be developed through any appropriate method or combination of methods. In making its determination, a Licensee may consider evidence including: recognised qualifications or programmes of learning; other formal qualifications or programmes of study; work-based or experiential learning; in-employment education and training; self-directed learning; technical reports or other technical evidence; technical interviews, presentations, examinations or other appropriate assessment methods; relevant existing Engineering Council registration; or any combination of these.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"may": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-001", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 28 (closing sentence)", "v44Paras": [28], "num": "5.4", "html": "<p>Regardless of how underpinning knowledge and understanding are developed or demonstrated, Licensees must satisfy themselves that the applicant’s knowledge and understanding are sufficient, relevant and applicable to their engineering practice.</p>", "text": "Regardless of how underpinning knowledge and understanding are developed or demonstrated, Licensees must satisfy themselves that the applicant’s knowledge and understanding are sufficient, relevant and applicable to their engineering practice.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "subsections": [{"title": "Recognised programmes", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-004", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 28, 37, 73", "v44Paras": [28, 37, 73], "num": "5.5", "html": "<p>Licensees may accept completion of a recognised programme as evidence of the underpinning knowledge and understanding required, provided they have satisfied themselves that it meets the requirement for the category of registration sought (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-001\" data-ref=\"UKU-001\">UKU-001</a>). Where it meets the requirement only in part, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-008\" data-ref=\"UKU-008\">UKU-008</a> applies.</p>", "text": "Licensees may accept completion of a recognised programme as evidence of the underpinning knowledge and understanding required, provided they have satisfied themselves that it meets the requirement for the category of registration sought (UKU-001). Where it meets the requirement only in part, UKU-008 applies.", "refs": ["UKU-001", "UKU-008"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-006", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 28, 37, 39", "v44Paras": [28, 37, 39], "num": "5.6", "html": "<p>Where a Licensee relies on a recognised programme as evidence of underpinning knowledge and understanding, it must ensure that:</p><ul><li>the level of the recognised programme held matches or exceeds the level of registration sought by the applicant; and</li><li>the programme is relevant to the engineering discipline(s) covered by the Licensee.</li></ul>", "text": "Where a Licensee relies on a recognised programme as evidence of underpinning knowledge and understanding, it must ensure that: the level of the recognised programme held matches or exceeds the level of registration sought by the applicant; and the programme is relevant to the engineering discipline(s) covered by the Licensee.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 71", "v44Paras": [71], "id": "EX-05-02", "html": "<p>The Engineering Council may enter agreements with overseas bodies on mutual recognition of recognised qualifications following consultation with Licensees.</p>", "text": "The Engineering Council may enter agreements with overseas bodies on mutual recognition of recognised qualifications following consultation with Licensees.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-071", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 71", "v44Paras": [71], "num": "5.7", "html": "<p>Licensees must treat such qualifications as equivalent to accredited UK qualifications for registration purposes, subject to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-004\" data-ref=\"UKU-004\">UKU-004</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-006\" data-ref=\"UKU-006\">UKU-006</a>.</p>", "text": "Licensees must treat such qualifications as equivalent to accredited UK qualifications for registration purposes, subject to UKU-004 and UKU-006.", "refs": ["UKU-004", "UKU-006"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S05.1", "anchor": "recognised-programmes"}, {"title": "Gaps and additional assessment", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-008", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 18, 26, 28, 32", "v44Paras": [18, 26, 28, 32], "num": "5.8", "html": "<p>Licensees must inform applicants of the outcome of the assessment of their underpinning knowledge and understanding. Where the requirements of the Knowledge Benchmark have not been demonstrated in full, Licensees must:</p><ul><li>identify the outstanding requirements and tell the applicant what they are;</li><li>provide the applicant with an appropriate opportunity to demonstrate them through additional evidence or assessment; and</li><li>ensure that any additional assessment is reasonable and proportionate to the requirements that remain to be demonstrated.</li></ul>", "text": "Licensees must inform applicants of the outcome of the assessment of their underpinning knowledge and understanding. Where the requirements of the Knowledge Benchmark have not been demonstrated in full, Licensees must: identify the outstanding requirements and tell the applicant what they are; provide the applicant with an appropriate opportunity to demonstrate them through additional evidence or assessment; and ensure that any additional assessment is reasonable and proportionate to the requirements that remain to be demonstrated.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-009", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 28-33", "v44Paras": [28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33], "num": "5.9", "html": "<p>Licensees may use a technical report, technical interview, presentation, examination or other appropriate assessment method where additional assessment is necessary to determine whether an applicant has demonstrated the required underpinning knowledge and understanding.</p>", "text": "Licensees may use a technical report, technical interview, presentation, examination or other appropriate assessment method where additional assessment is necessary to determine whether an applicant has demonstrated the required underpinning knowledge and understanding.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S05.2", "anchor": "gaps-and-additional-assessment"}, {"title": "Technical report", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-017", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 29, 31, 32", "v44Paras": [29, 31, 32], "num": "5.10", "html": "<p>A Licensee may ask an applicant to submit a technical report as a means of demonstrating knowledge and understanding. This report must demonstrate that the applicant has the knowledge and understanding necessary to underpin the UK-SPEC competences for the category of registration sought. Its content must be technical; a pure management study is not acceptable.</p>", "text": "A Licensee may ask an applicant to submit a technical report as a means of demonstrating knowledge and understanding. This report must demonstrate that the applicant has the knowledge and understanding necessary to underpin the UK-SPEC competences for the category of registration sought. Its content must be technical; a pure management study is not acceptable.", "refs": [], "status": "retained", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-018", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 29, 31, 32", "v44Paras": [29, 31, 32], "num": "5.11", "html": "<p>The technical report must be assessed by peer review (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>). The assessors of the report and the interview must be two registrants, suitably qualified and trained to assess underpinning knowledge and understanding. An IEng or CEng applicant whose technical report is assessed as satisfactory must proceed to a technical report interview. A report of the technical report assessment may be made available to the Professional Review assessors. While the Professional Review Interview may take place back-to-back with the technical report assessment interview, the two processes must be separately identified and documented.</p>", "text": "The technical report must be assessed by peer review (V5-074). The assessors of the report and the interview must be two registrants, suitably qualified and trained to assess underpinning knowledge and understanding. An IEng or CEng applicant whose technical report is assessed as satisfactory must proceed to a technical report interview. A report of the technical report assessment may be made available to the Professional Review assessors. While the Professional Review Interview may take place back-to-back with the technical report assessment interview, the two processes must be separately identified and documented.", "refs": ["V5-074"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 4, "may": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S05.3", "anchor": "technical-report"}, {"title": "Records", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-014", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 3, 24, 25, 31, 32", "v44Paras": [3, 24, 25, 31, 32], "num": "5.12", "html": "<p>Licensees must maintain sufficient records of:</p><ul><li>the evidence considered;</li><li>any additional assessment undertaken;</li><li>the determination reached;</li><li>any outstanding requirements identified; and</li><li>the outcome communicated to the applicant.</li></ul>", "text": "Licensees must maintain sufficient records of: the evidence considered; any additional assessment undertaken; the determination reached; any outstanding requirements identified; and the outcome communicated to the applicant.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S05.4", "anchor": "records"}, {"title": "Interim registration", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-011", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 15, 32", "v44Paras": [15, 32], "num": "5.13", "html": "<p>Licensees may admit applicants to the Interim Section of the Register where they have determined that the applicant has demonstrated the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for the relevant registration category, but has not yet met all requirements for final-stage registration. An individual must not be registered in both the final-stage and interim sections of any one category of the Register.</p>", "text": "Licensees may admit applicants to the Interim Section of the Register where they have determined that the applicant has demonstrated the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for the relevant registration category, but has not yet met all requirements for final-stage registration. An individual must not be registered in both the final-stage and interim sections of any one category of the Register.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must not": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "UKU-012", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 15; new requirements", "v44Paras": [15], "num": "5.14", "html": "<p>Interim registration should be awarded for a defined period. Licensees must have arrangements for reviewing an applicant's status at the end of that period and determining whether Interim Registration should be renewed or allowed to expire.</p>", "text": "Interim registration should be awarded for a defined period. Licensees must have arrangements for reviewing an applicant's status at the end of that period and determining whether Interim Registration should be renewed or allowed to expire.", "refs": [], "status": "new", "modality": {"must": 1, "should": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S05.5", "anchor": "interim-registration"}], "num": 5, "id": "S05", "anchor": "assessment-of-underpinning-knowledge-and-understanding", "licence": "registration"}, {"title": "The Professional Review", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-019", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 19", "v44Paras": [19], "num": "6.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must assess the competence and commitment of every applicant for registration, in any registration category, through a Professional Review conducted by the Licensee through which they are applying for registration.</p>", "text": "Licensees must assess the competence and commitment of every applicant for registration, in any registration category, through a Professional Review conducted by the Licensee through which they are applying for registration.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-020", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 20", "v44Paras": [20], "num": "6.2", "html": "<p>The Professional Review must include two components:</p><ul><li>a review of documentary evidence; and</li><li>an interview.</li></ul><p>Licensees may add other components to the Professional Review.</p>", "text": "The Professional Review must include two components: a review of documentary evidence; and an interview. Licensees may add other components to the Professional Review.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-023", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 23", "v44Paras": [23], "num": "6.3", "html": "<p>The application, supporting documentation and interview must be in English, subject only to the provisions of the Welsh Language Act 1993. Another language may be used purely for the clarification of a word or point, to enable the interview to continue.</p>", "text": "The application, supporting documentation and interview must be in English, subject only to the provisions of the Welsh Language Act 1993. Another language may be used purely for the clarification of a word or point, to enable the interview to continue.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [{"title": "Review of documentary evidence", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "id": "EX-06-01", "html": "<p>The review of documentary evidence is the first point at which peers assess the applicant’s competence and commitment.</p>", "text": "The review of documentary evidence is the first point at which peers assess the applicant’s competence and commitment.", "refs": [], "status": "new"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-077", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 20", "v44Paras": [20], "num": "6.4", "html": "<p>Licensees must not progress applicants to interview without a recommendation from the assessors of the documentary evidence.</p>", "text": "Licensees must not progress applicants to interview without a recommendation from the assessors of the documentary evidence.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must not": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S06.1", "anchor": "review-of-documentary-evidence"}, {"title": "Interview", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-078", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 20", "v44Paras": [20], "num": "6.5", "html": "<p>Licensees must interview all applicants for final-stage CEng or IEng registration, except those exempted under <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-072\" data-ref=\"V5-072\">V5-072</a>; and may interview EngTech and ICT<em>Tech</em> applicants at their discretion.</p>", "text": "Licensees must interview all applicants for final-stage CEng or IEng registration, except those exempted under V5-072; and may interview EngTech and ICT Tech applicants at their discretion.", "refs": ["V5-072"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1", "T3"]}], "id": "S06.2", "anchor": "interview"}, {"title": "Applicants covered by international agreements", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 72", "v44Paras": [72], "id": "EX-06-02", "html": "<p>Following consultation with Licensees, the Engineering Council may enter into agreements with overseas bodies on mutual recognition of overseas titles, or authorise agreements made by Licensees. The scope of each agreement, and the exemptions it gives, are set out in the agreement itself. A list of current agreements is available from the Engineering Council.</p>", "text": "Following consultation with Licensees, the Engineering Council may enter into agreements with overseas bodies on mutual recognition of overseas titles, or authorise agreements made by Licensees. The scope of each agreement, and the exemptions it gives, are set out in the agreement itself. A list of current agreements is available from the Engineering Council.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-072", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 72", "v44Paras": [72], "num": "6.6", "html": "<p>Licensees must have a procedure for applying the agreements in which they participate, and must use it to exempt appropriate applicants from all or part of the registration requirements according to each agreement’s scope.</p>", "text": "Licensees must have a procedure for applying the agreements in which they participate, and must use it to exempt appropriate applicants from all or part of the registration requirements according to each agreement’s scope.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": []}], "id": "S06.3", "anchor": "applicants-covered-by-international-agreements"}, {"title": "Assessors and reports", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-022", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 22", "v44Paras": [22], "num": "6.7", "html": "<p>Both elements of the Professional Review must be conducted by two peers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>). The assessors must be final-stage Engineering Council registrants, at least one of whom must be at or above the registration category in which the applicant is seeking registration.</p>", "text": "Both elements of the Professional Review must be conducted by two peers (V5-074). The assessors must be final-stage Engineering Council registrants, at least one of whom must be at or above the registration category in which the applicant is seeking registration.", "refs": ["V5-074"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3}, "tables": ["T1", "T3"]}, {"type": "table", "caption": "Table 3: who may assess whom", "header": ["Category sought", "Both assessors", "At least one assessor", "Clauses"], "rows": [["EngTech or ICT<em>Tech</em>", "Final-stage Engineering Council registrants", "Any category", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-022\" data-ref=\"V5-022\">V5-022</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>; <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-078\" data-ref=\"V5-078\">V5-078</a> (interview at the Licensee's discretion)"], ["IEng", "Final-stage Engineering Council registrants", "IEng or CEng", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-022\" data-ref=\"V5-022\">V5-022</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>; <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-078\" data-ref=\"V5-078\">V5-078</a> (interview required)"], ["CEng", "Final-stage Engineering Council registrants", "CEng", "<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-022\" data-ref=\"V5-022\">V5-022</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>; <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-078\" data-ref=\"V5-078\">V5-078</a> (interview required)"]], "id": "T3", "rowAppliesTo": [["V5-022", "V5-074", "V5-078"], ["V5-022", "V5-074", "V5-078"], ["V5-022", "V5-074", "V5-078"]], "appliesTo": ["V5-022", "V5-074", "V5-078"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-024", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 24", "v44Paras": [24], "num": "6.8", "html": "<p>The assessors must complete a summary report, or reports, covering each component of the Professional Review, with a recommendation for each applicant. The report or reports must set out the assessors’ professional judgment of whether the applicant has demonstrated the competence and commitment required by the standard at the appropriate level.</p>", "text": "The assessors must complete a summary report, or reports, covering each component of the Professional Review, with a recommendation for each applicant. The report or reports must set out the assessors’ professional judgment of whether the applicant has demonstrated the competence and commitment required by the standard at the appropriate level.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "id": "S06.4", "anchor": "assessors-and-reports"}], "num": 6, "id": "S06", "anchor": "the-professional-review", "licence": "registration"}, {"title": "Registration decision", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-025", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 25", "v44Paras": [25], "num": "7.1", "html": "<p>The decision whether or not to accept an applicant for registration must be made by the Licensee’s committee responsible for registration. The committee must base its decision on the reports from the Professional Review, against the standard adopted by the Licensee under <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-012\" data-ref=\"V5-012\">V5-012</a>. Its decisions, including recommendations, justifications, feedback and moderation, must be documented, transparent and auditable. Details of any international agreements or regulations applied and exemptions given must be recorded.</p><p>The committee must not award a registration title unless both assessors recommend it. Each Licensee must have a procedure for cases where the decision is not to accept an applicant on the basis of the Professional Review reports. The committee may recommend that the applicant repeat one or more components. Conditional passes are not permitted.</p>", "text": "The decision whether or not to accept an applicant for registration must be made by the Licensee’s committee responsible for registration. The committee must base its decision on the reports from the Professional Review, against the standard adopted by the Licensee under V5-012. Its decisions, including recommendations, justifications, feedback and moderation, must be documented, transparent and auditable. Details of any international agreements or regulations applied and exemptions given must be recorded. The committee must not award a registration title unless both assessors recommend it. Each Licensee must have a procedure for cases where the decision is not to accept an applicant on the basis of the Professional Review reports. The committee may recommend that the applicant repeat one or more components. Conditional passes are not permitted.", "refs": ["V5-012"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must not": 1, "must": 5, "may": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-026", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 26", "v44Paras": [26], "num": "7.2", "html": "<p>Licensees must inform the applicant of the outcome and, if unsuccessful, advise them about appropriate action to address deficiencies.</p>", "text": "Licensees must inform the applicant of the outcome and, if unsuccessful, advise them about appropriate action to address deficiencies.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": ["T1"]}], "subsections": [], "num": 7, "id": "S07", "anchor": "registration-decision", "licence": "registration"}, {"title": "Appeals", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-027", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 27", "v44Paras": [27], "num": "8.1", "html": "<p>Each Licensee must have an appeals procedure available to unsuccessful applicants. Unsuccessful applicants may appeal against the legitimacy of the process followed during the review of UKU and/or Professional Review, but not against the decision itself. Appeals must be considered by an Appeals Committee composed of registrants who did not materially participate in the appellant’s Professional Review.</p><p>An Appeals Committee may recommend that the applicant repeat one or more components of the Professional Review, but may not summarily reverse the recommendation of an earlier assessment. The final decision remains with the committee responsible for registration.</p>", "text": "Each Licensee must have an appeals procedure available to unsuccessful applicants. Unsuccessful applicants may appeal against the legitimacy of the process followed during the review of UKU and/or Professional Review, but not against the decision itself. Appeals must be considered by an Appeals Committee composed of registrants who did not materially participate in the appellant’s Professional Review. An Appeals Committee may recommend that the applicant repeat one or more components of the Professional Review, but may not summarily reverse the recommendation of an earlier assessment. The final decision remains with the committee responsible for registration.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 3}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [], "num": 8, "id": "S08", "anchor": "appeals", "licence": "core"}, {"title": "Recognition of programmes of learning", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 34, 37", "v44Paras": [34, 37], "id": "EX-09-01", "html": "<p>Programmes of learning include degrees, apprenticeships, national and vocational learning, and other qualifications, including those led by employers and Licensees. Recognition means that a programme fully or partially delivers the learning outcomes and/or delivers some or all of the competences for the relevant category of the Register set out in UK-SPEC.</p>", "text": "Programmes of learning include degrees, apprenticeships, national and vocational learning, and other qualifications, including those led by employers and Licensees. Recognition means that a programme fully or partially delivers the learning outcomes and/or delivers some or all of the competences for the relevant category of the Register set out in UK-SPEC.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 34, 37", "v44Paras": [34, 37], "id": "EX-09-02", "html": "<p>Recognition may take the form of approval or accreditation. These are used to recognise programmes that provide all or some of the underpinning knowledge and understanding and/or develop competence for eventual registration in a particular category.</p>", "text": "Recognition may take the form of approval or accreditation. These are used to recognise programmes that provide all or some of the underpinning knowledge and understanding and/or develop competence for eventual registration in a particular category.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 34, 37", "v44Paras": [34, 37], "id": "EX-09-03", "html": "<p>Recognition, approval and accreditation are defined in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#DEF-005\" data-ref=\"DEF-005\">DEF-005</a> to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#DEF-007\" data-ref=\"DEF-007\">DEF-007</a>. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#T4\" data-ref=\"T4\">Table 4</a> summarises how approval and accreditation differ, and how accreditation of an IPD scheme compares.</p>", "text": "Recognition, approval and accreditation are defined in DEF-005 to DEF-007. Table 4 summarises how approval and accreditation differ, and how accreditation of an IPD scheme compares.", "refs": ["DEF-005", "DEF-007", "T4"], "status": "new"}, {"type": "table", "caption": "Table 4: approval and accreditation compared", "header": ["", "Programme Approval", "Programme Accreditation", "IPD Scheme Accreditation"], "rows": [["What it attests", "A programme’s content: its overall design, depth and range of coverage, and the validity and reliability of learner assessment", "Everything approval attests, plus that the programme’s delivery is recognised at a specific site or sites", "A scheme’s overall design and content, and the validity and reliability of its assessments; and that its delivery is recognised at a specific site or sites, or at a specific organisation"], ["Measured against", "Learning outcomes at a specific level, as described in AHEP and AAQA, or competence at a level described in AAQA", "As for approval", "The competence and commitment standards adopted by the Licensee, not Learning Outcomes"], ["What it recognises", "Underpinning knowledge and understanding; can also recognise competence development", "As for approval", "The opportunity to develop and demonstrate competence and commitment. Does not in itself demonstrate underpinning knowledge and understanding"], ["Visit", "If necessary (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-045\" data-ref=\"V5-045\">V5-045</a>)", "Required; may be remote in certain circumstances, or waived (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-040\" data-ref=\"V5-040\">V5-040</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-056\" data-ref=\"V5-056\">V5-056</a>)", "Required; remote re-accreditation possible after a risk-based assessment (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#IPD-009\" data-ref=\"IPD-009\">IPD-009</a>)"], ["Listed on the recognised programmes database", "Yes (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-054\" data-ref=\"V5-054\">V5-054</a>)", "Yes (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-054\" data-ref=\"V5-054\">V5-054</a>)", "No"], ["Licence required", "Recognition (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-035\" data-ref=\"V5-035\">V5-035</a>)", "Recognition (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-035\" data-ref=\"V5-035\">V5-035</a>)", "IPD accreditation (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#IPD-001\" data-ref=\"IPD-001\">IPD-001</a>)"]], "id": "T4"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-035", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 35, 41", "v44Paras": [35, 41], "num": "9.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must hold the relevant licence to recognise programmes for registration purposes. Licensees that recognise programmes must appoint a committee responsible for recognition, which decides whether a programme will be recognised on the basis of the report of the accreditation or approval panel. Licensees must assess programmes against the learning outcomes in AHEP and AAQA, or against their own learning outcomes derived from and meeting those in AHEP and AAQA.</p>", "text": "Licensees must hold the relevant licence to recognise programmes for registration purposes. Licensees that recognise programmes must appoint a committee responsible for recognition, which decides whether a programme will be recognised on the basis of the report of the accreditation or approval panel. Licensees must assess programmes against the learning outcomes in AHEP and AAQA, or against their own learning outcomes derived from and meeting those in AHEP and AAQA.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-036", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 36", "v44Paras": [36], "num": "9.2", "html": "<p>Recognition may attest to acquisition of both competence and underpinning knowledge and understanding, for example in integrated programmes. Licensees recognising such programmes must adopt competence statements derived from and meeting those in AAQA.</p>", "text": "Recognition may attest to acquisition of both competence and underpinning knowledge and understanding, for example in integrated programmes. Licensees recognising such programmes must adopt competence statements derived from and meeting those in AAQA.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [{"title": "Recognition panels, criteria and decision-making", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-042", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 42", "v44Paras": [42], "num": "9.3", "html": "<p>Licensees undertaking recognition must establish detailed criteria and procedures, whether applied alone or through a joint board (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#DEF-008\" data-ref=\"DEF-008\">DEF-008</a>), specifying:</p><ul><li>selection and training of assessors;</li><li>constitution of panels;</li><li>a balance of academic and industry assessors;</li><li>reasonable steps to avoid conflicts of interest;</li><li>panel experience relevant to the registration category;</li><li>the required provider submission;</li><li>any charges;</li><li>outline visit agendas;</li><li>judgment criteria, including mapped learning outcomes;</li><li>further consultation, decisions and feedback; and</li><li>the processes by which appeals against decisions may be considered, and</li><li>notification of decisions to the Engineering Council.</li></ul>", "text": "Licensees undertaking recognition must establish detailed criteria and procedures, whether applied alone or through a joint board (DEF-008), specifying: selection and training of assessors; constitution of panels; a balance of academic and industry assessors; reasonable steps to avoid conflicts of interest; panel experience relevant to the registration category; the required provider submission; any charges; outline visit agendas; judgment criteria, including mapped learning outcomes; further consultation, decisions and feedback; and the processes by which appeals against decisions may be considered, and notification of decisions to the Engineering Council.", "refs": ["DEF-008"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-043", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 43", "v44Paras": [43], "num": "9.4", "html": "<p>In making a judgment, Licensees must consider evidence from a range of indicators, including:</p><ul><li>programme learning outcomes;</li><li>teaching and learning processes;</li><li>assessment strategies;</li><li>human, physical and material resources;</li><li>quality assurance arrangements;</li><li>feedback from students;</li><li>responses to previous recommendations and requirements; and</li><li>entry to the programme and support for cohort entry extremes.</li></ul><p>For degree accreditation, evidence must include the awarding institution’s regulations on progression and awards. For approval of a formal qualification, evidence must include clearly defined, appropriately technical outcomes and satisfactory quality assurance arrangements.</p>", "text": "In making a judgment, Licensees must consider evidence from a range of indicators, including: programme learning outcomes; teaching and learning processes; assessment strategies; human, physical and material resources; quality assurance arrangements; feedback from students; responses to previous recommendations and requirements; and entry to the programme and support for cohort entry extremes. For degree accreditation, evidence must include the awarding institution’s regulations on progression and awards. For approval of a formal qualification, evidence must include clearly defined, appropriately technical outcomes and satisfactory quality assurance arrangements.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-044", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 44", "v44Paras": [44], "num": "9.5", "html": "<p>Any decision by a Licensee to recognise a programme for which it is itself the awarding institution must be submitted to the Registration Standards Committee for authorisation.</p>", "text": "Any decision by a Licensee to recognise a programme for which it is itself the awarding institution must be submitted to the Registration Standards Committee for authorisation.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-045", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 45", "v44Paras": [45], "num": "9.6", "html": "<p>In considering applications for recognition, Licensees must:</p><ul><li>recognise only programmes providing awards based on clearly defined learning outcomes;</li><li>ensure the programme is at an appropriate level in the applicable UK or international qualifications framework;</li><li>monitor the accuracy of published information about recognition status and registration;</li><li>visit the awarding institution if necessary; and</li><li>ensure that, where recognition attests to acquisition of competence, the programme covers the relevant competence standards in UK-SPEC or AAQA.</li></ul>", "text": "In considering applications for recognition, Licensees must: recognise only programmes providing awards based on clearly defined learning outcomes; ensure the programme is at an appropriate level in the applicable UK or international qualifications framework; monitor the accuracy of published information about recognition status and registration; visit the awarding institution if necessary; and ensure that, where recognition attests to acquisition of competence, the programme covers the relevant competence standards in UK-SPEC or AAQA.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-046", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 46", "v44Paras": [46], "num": "9.7", "html": "<p>For accreditation of engineering degree programmes, Licensees must ensure that all students achieve all AHEP learning outcomes regardless of options taken, and the maximum credits permitted as 'failed' by university regulations. Evidence must be provided before accreditation. Condonement of modules delivering AHEP learning outcomes is not allowed. Compensation limits apply, major project modules must not be compensated, and the minimum compensated mark must be no more than ten percentage points below the nominal pass mark or equivalent.</p>", "text": "For accreditation of engineering degree programmes, Licensees must ensure that all students achieve all AHEP learning outcomes regardless of options taken, and the maximum credits permitted as 'failed' by university regulations. Evidence must be provided before accreditation. Condonement of modules delivering AHEP learning outcomes is not allowed. Compensation limits apply, major project modules must not be compensated, and the minimum compensated mark must be no more than ten percentage points below the nominal pass mark or equivalent.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must not": 1, "must": 3}, "tables": []}], "id": "S09.1", "anchor": "recognition-panels-criteria-and-decision-making"}, {"title": "Recognition outcomes and renewal", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 47 (the outcome categories)", "v44Paras": [47], "id": "EX-09-04", "html": "<p>A programme may be recognised as fully or partially meeting the learning outcomes at the requisite level. Recognition that partially meets the learning outcomes ('partial recognition') means that a recognised programme delivers a defined subset of the required learning outcomes and underpinning knowledge specified for a particular professional title. It confirms that the programme delivers part of the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for a category of registration, and that holders will need to demonstrate the remainder by other means (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#UKU-008\" data-ref=\"UKU-008\">UKU-008</a>).</p>", "text": "A programme may be recognised as fully or partially meeting the learning outcomes at the requisite level. Recognition that partially meets the learning outcomes ('partial recognition') means that a recognised programme delivers a defined subset of the required learning outcomes and underpinning knowledge specified for a particular professional title. It confirms that the programme delivers part of the underpinning knowledge and understanding required for a category of registration, and that holders will need to demonstrate the remainder by other means (UKU-008).", "refs": ["UKU-008"], "status": "new"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 para 47 (the outcome categories)", "v44Paras": [47], "id": "EX-09-05", "html": "<p>Recognition may cover underpinning knowledge and understanding only or, for integrated programmes, competence as well (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-036\" data-ref=\"V5-036\">V5-036</a>).</p>", "text": "Recognition may cover underpinning knowledge and understanding only or, for integrated programmes, competence as well (V5-036).", "refs": ["V5-036"], "status": "new"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-047", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 47", "v44Paras": [47], "num": "9.8", "html": "<p>The committee responsible for recognition must decide to:</p><ul><li>recognise the programme;</li><li>recognise the programme subject to specified constraints and/or specified modifications within a set timetable (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-079\" data-ref=\"V5-079\">V5-079</a>); or</li><li>not recognise the programme.</li></ul><p>Where it recognises a programme, the committee must state whether the programme fully or partially meets the learning outcomes at the requisite level, and whether it additionally meets, fully or partially, the competence requirement for the relevant category of UK-SPEC.</p>", "text": "The committee responsible for recognition must decide to: recognise the programme; recognise the programme subject to specified constraints and/or specified modifications within a set timetable (V5-079); or not recognise the programme. Where it recognises a programme, the committee must state whether the programme fully or partially meets the learning outcomes at the requisite level, and whether it additionally meets, fully or partially, the competence requirement for the relevant category of UK-SPEC.", "refs": ["V5-079"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-079", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 47 (fourth bullet)", "v44Paras": [47], "num": "9.9", "html": "<p>Where the committee grants recognition subject to constraints or modifications, the Licensee must specify them, and the timetable for meeting them, in its decision, and must notify the Engineering Council about any constraints which would restrict the eligibility of award holders.</p>", "text": "Where the committee grants recognition subject to constraints or modifications, the Licensee must specify them, and the timetable for meeting them, in its decision, and must notify the Engineering Council about any constraints which would restrict the eligibility of award holders.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-048", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 48", "v44Paras": [48], "num": "9.10", "html": "<p>Licensees must recognise programmes for a fixed period of not more than five years.</p>", "text": "Licensees must recognise programmes for a fixed period of not more than five years.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-049", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 49", "v44Paras": [49], "num": "9.11", "html": "<p>Where a recognised programme’s curriculum does not change during the five-year period, the Licensee may renew recognition if it is satisfied that the programme remains relevant and meets the required standards.</p>", "text": "Where a recognised programme’s curriculum does not change during the five-year period, the Licensee may renew recognition if it is satisfied that the programme remains relevant and meets the required standards.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-080", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 49", "v44Paras": [49], "num": "9.12", "html": "<p>In exceptional circumstances, a Licensee may extend recognition by a maximum of one year, once per recognition period, without a full renewal process. Before doing so, the Licensee must satisfy itself, and record, that the risk of extending recognition is acceptable.</p>", "text": "In exceptional circumstances, a Licensee may extend recognition by a maximum of one year, once per recognition period, without a full renewal process. Before doing so, the Licensee must satisfy itself, and record, that the risk of extending recognition is acceptable.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-050", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 50", "v44Paras": [50], "num": "9.13", "html": "<p>Recognition may be backdated to allow cohorts whose work was reviewed as part of the recognition process to benefit. Backdating should apply immediately before forward recognition. Earlier backdating may be considered for RSC authorisation case by case; decisions must be documented, transparent and auditable.</p>", "text": "Recognition may be backdated to allow cohorts whose work was reviewed as part of the recognition process to benefit. Backdating should apply immediately before forward recognition. Earlier backdating may be considered for RSC authorisation case by case; decisions must be documented, transparent and auditable.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "should": 1, "may": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-052", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 52", "v44Paras": [52], "num": "9.14", "html": "<p>Programmes without an output cohort may be recognised. Licensees must monitor their output until at least one cohort has completed the programme and review recognition accordingly.</p>", "text": "Programmes without an output cohort may be recognised. Licensees must monitor their output until at least one cohort has completed the programme and review recognition accordingly.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-051", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 51", "v44Paras": [51], "num": "9.15", "html": "<p>Licensees must require providers or awarding institutions to inform them of major programme changes during the recognition period.</p>", "text": "Licensees must require providers or awarding institutions to inform them of major programme changes during the recognition period.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-053", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 53", "v44Paras": [53], "num": "9.16", "html": "<p>Renewal must use the same process as initial recognition</p>", "text": "Renewal must use the same process as initial recognition", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-054", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 54", "v44Paras": [54], "num": "9.17", "html": "<p>Licensees must add all programmes they recognise to the Engineering Council’s recognised qualifications database, and must ensure that the data they have listed is current, complete and accurate. Recognition outside these Regulations must be authorised by RSC before database entry.</p>", "text": "Licensees must add all programmes they recognise to the Engineering Council’s recognised qualifications database, and must ensure that the data they have listed is current, complete and accurate. Recognition outside these Regulations must be authorised by RSC before database entry.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-055", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 55", "v44Paras": [55], "num": "9.18", "html": "<p>Licensees may remove recognition from a programme before the end of the recognition period, in which case they must update the recognised qualifications database promptly and alert the Engineering Council as early as possible. They must also require the awarding organisation or provider to notify affected individuals, explaining support available, implications for future registration and anything additional they must achieve.</p>", "text": "Licensees may remove recognition from a programme before the end of the recognition period, in which case they must update the recognised qualifications database promptly and alert the Engineering Council as early as possible. They must also require the awarding organisation or provider to notify affected individuals, explaining support available, implications for future registration and anything additional they must achieve.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 3, "may": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S09.2", "anchor": "recognition-outcomes-and-renewal"}, {"title": "Accreditation visits", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "V5-040", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 40", "v44Paras": [40], "num": "9.19", "html": "<p>The accreditation process must include a visit to the delivery site. Visits may be remote in certain circumstances. Where the same programme is delivered at multiple sites, the Licensee must satisfy itself that standards are met at each site for which accreditation applies; additional visits may be required.</p>", "text": "The accreditation process must include a visit to the delivery site. Visits may be remote in certain circumstances. Where the same programme is delivered at multiple sites, the Licensee must satisfy itself that standards are met at each site for which accreditation applies; additional visits may be required.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-059", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 59", "v44Paras": [59], "num": "9.20", "html": "<p>Where part of a programme is delivered by another provider, the Licensee must visit that provider as part of accreditation unless it can assure itself that systems ensure delivery of the adopted learning outcomes, or that part does not contribute to the final award. The decision applies only to that exercise and must be recorded.</p>", "text": "Where part of a programme is delivered by another provider, the Licensee must visit that provider as part of accreditation unless it can assure itself that systems ensure delivery of the adopted learning outcomes, or that part does not contribute to the final award. The decision applies only to that exercise and must be recorded.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "V5-056", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 56-58", "v44Paras": [56, 57, 58], "num": "9.21", "html": "<p>A Licensee may waive an accreditation visit where the programme has significant commonality with programmes it already accredits and sufficient evidence shows that matters normally reviewed during a visit are satisfactory. The detailed conditions for waiving a visit are set out in AHEP and AAQA.</p>", "text": "A Licensee may waive an accreditation visit where the programme has significant commonality with programmes it already accredits and sufficient evidence shows that matters normally reviewed during a visit are satisfactory. The detailed conditions for waiving a visit are set out in AHEP and AAQA.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"may": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S09.3", "anchor": "accreditation-visits"}], "num": 9, "id": "S09", "anchor": "recognition-of-programmes-of-learning", "licence": "recognition"}, {"title": "Accreditation of Initial Professional Development", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 60, 62, 65", "v44Paras": [60, 62, 65], "id": "EX-10-01", "html": "<p>The nature, purpose and value of IPD, and the processes for accrediting structured IPD schemes, are described in the IPD Policy Statement.</p>", "text": "The nature, purpose and value of IPD, and the processes for accrediting structured IPD schemes, are described in the IPD Policy Statement.", "refs": [], "status": "retained"}, {"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 60, 62, 65", "v44Paras": [60, 62, 65], "id": "EX-10-02", "html": "<p>Accreditation of an IPD scheme is the formal recognition that a provider's scheme provides engineers or technicians with the opportunity to develop and demonstrate the competence and commitment required by the applicable UK-SPEC standard, enabling them to apply for Professional Review.</p>", "text": "Accreditation of an IPD scheme is the formal recognition that a provider's scheme provides engineers or technicians with the opportunity to develop and demonstrate the competence and commitment required by the applicable UK-SPEC standard, enabling them to apply for Professional Review.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-001", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 62", "v44Paras": [62], "num": "10.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must hold the relevant licence to accredit IPD schemes.</p>", "text": "Licensees must hold the relevant licence to accredit IPD schemes.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [{"title": "Requirements for Licensees", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-005", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 63; paras 41-43", "v44Paras": [41, 42, 43, 63], "num": "10.2", "html": "<p>A Licensee that accredits IPD schemes must publish and maintain documented policies, procedures and criteria for accreditation.</p>", "text": "A Licensee that accredits IPD schemes must publish and maintain documented policies, procedures and criteria for accreditation.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-006", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 63; paras 41-43", "v44Paras": [41, 42, 43, 63], "num": "10.3", "html": "<p>Licensees holding a licence to accredit an IPD scheme must:</p><ul><li>publish criteria against which IPD schemes will be assessed;</li><li>define the information and evidence required from providers seeking accreditation;</li><li>recruit, train and approve suitably competent accreditation assessors;</li><li>define the standards and methods used to assess applications;</li><li>document the accreditation process, including accreditation visits;</li><li>ensure that accreditation decisions are based on an auditable peer review process (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#V5-074\" data-ref=\"V5-074\">V5-074</a>);</li><li>define the range of possible accreditation outcomes;</li><li>define how accreditation recommendations, judgements and decisions are reached and recorded; and</li><li>maintain records of accreditation activity and decisions.</li></ul>", "text": "Licensees holding a licence to accredit an IPD scheme must: publish criteria against which IPD schemes will be assessed; define the information and evidence required from providers seeking accreditation; recruit, train and approve suitably competent accreditation assessors; define the standards and methods used to assess applications; document the accreditation process, including accreditation visits; ensure that accreditation decisions are based on an auditable peer review process (V5-074); define the range of possible accreditation outcomes; define how accreditation recommendations, judgements and decisions are reached and recorded; and maintain records of accreditation activity and decisions.", "refs": ["V5-074"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S10.1", "anchor": "requirements-for-licensees"}, {"title": "Requirements for accredited IPD schemes", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-007", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 60, 62, 65", "v44Paras": [60, 62, 65], "num": "10.4", "html": "<p>To be accredited, an IPD scheme must demonstrate that it:</p><ul><li>is structured, documented and implemented;</li><li>provides participants with a clear understanding of the requirements for professional registration, including:<ul><li>underpinning knowledge and understanding (where applicable);</li><li>competence development against the relevant UK-SPEC standard; and</li><li>preparation for Professional Review;</li></ul></li><li>provides opportunities for participants to achieve the relevant UK-SPEC competence and commitment requirements;</li><li>identifies the sites, functions or activities through which participants can gain the required experience and evidence;</li><li>has visible support from senior management;</li><li>defines clear roles, responsibilities and accountabilities for the operation and delivery of the scheme;</li><li>provides access to appropriate guidance and mentoring;</li><li>operates suitable quality assurance and continuous improvement processes;</li><li>maintains records that demonstrate participant progress and achievement against the relevant UK-SPEC standard; and</li><li>provides each participant with a portable record of achievement that contains sufficient evidence for use at Professional Review with any Licensee.</li></ul>", "text": "To be accredited, an IPD scheme must demonstrate that it: is structured, documented and implemented; provides participants with a clear understanding of the requirements for professional registration, including: underpinning knowledge and understanding (where applicable); competence development against the relevant UK-SPEC standard; and preparation for Professional Review; provides opportunities for participants to achieve the relevant UK-SPEC competence and commitment requirements; identifies the sites, functions or activities through which participants can gain the required experience and evidence; has visible support from senior management; defines clear roles, responsibilities and accountabilities for the operation and delivery of the scheme; provides access to appropriate guidance and mentoring; operates suitable quality assurance and continuous improvement processes; maintains records that demonstrate participant progress and achievement against the relevant UK-SPEC standard; and provides each participant with a portable record of achievement that contains sufficient evidence for use at Professional Review with any Licensee.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-008", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 60, 65; new role specificity", "v44Paras": [60, 65], "num": "10.5", "html": "<p>As a minimum, the scheme must define responsibilities for:</p><ul><li>a Scheme Sponsor;</li><li>a Scheme Manager or Scheme Owner;</li><li>Line Managers;</li><li>Mentors; and</li><li>Participants.</li></ul>", "text": "As a minimum, the scheme must define responsibilities for: a Scheme Sponsor; a Scheme Manager or Scheme Owner; Line Managers; Mentors; and Participants.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S10.2", "anchor": "requirements-for-accredited-ipd-schemes"}, {"title": "IPD accreditation visits", "blocks": [{"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-010", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 62-64", "v44Paras": [62, 63, 64], "num": "10.6", "html": "<p>The Licensee must ensure that any accreditation visit is undertaken by a suitably trained accreditation panel and that the outcomes, findings and decisions are recorded.</p>", "text": "The Licensee must ensure that any accreditation visit is undertaken by a suitably trained accreditation panel and that the outcomes, findings and decisions are recorded.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-013", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 62-64", "v44Paras": [62, 63, 64], "num": "10.7", "html": "<p>Where a scheme is delivered at more than one site or provider, the Licensee must satisfy itself that standards are met at each location for which accreditation will apply.</p>", "text": "Where a scheme is delivered at more than one site or provider, the Licensee must satisfy itself that standards are met at each location for which accreditation will apply.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-009", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 62-64", "v44Paras": [62, 63, 64], "num": "10.8", "html": "<p>Licensees may carry out a remote visit to re-accredit an IPD scheme, but must first undertake a risk assessment to inform a decision on whether an in-person element to the visit is required.</p>", "text": "Licensees may carry out a remote visit to re-accredit an IPD scheme, but must first undertake a risk assessment to inform a decision on whether an in-person element to the visit is required.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-011", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 62-64", "v44Paras": [62, 63, 64], "num": "10.9", "html": "<p>The Licensee must retain sufficient evidence to demonstrate compliance with its accreditation process and published criteria.</p>", "text": "The Licensee must retain sufficient evidence to demonstrate compliance with its accreditation process and published criteria.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "IPD-012", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 62-64", "v44Paras": [62, 63, 64], "num": "10.10", "html": "<p>Licensees may undertake accreditation assessment visits jointly with other Licensees where appropriate (a joint visit, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#DEF-009\" data-ref=\"DEF-009\">DEF-009</a>). Each Licensee must make its own decision on whether to accredit the scheme.</p>", "text": "Licensees may undertake accreditation assessment visits jointly with other Licensees where appropriate (a joint visit, DEF-009). Each Licensee must make its own decision on whether to accredit the scheme.", "refs": ["DEF-009"], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1, "may": 1}, "tables": []}], "id": "S10.3", "anchor": "ipd-accreditation-visits"}], "num": 10, "id": "S10", "anchor": "accreditation-of-initial-professional-development", "licence": "ipd"}, {"title": "Continuing Professional Development", "blocks": [{"type": "explanatory", "v44": "v4.4 paras 67, 69, 70", "v44Paras": [67, 69, 70], "id": "EX-11-01", "html": "<p>The nature, purpose and value of CPD for practising engineers, together with further information to support Licensees in meeting these requirements, are set out in the CPD Policy Statement. Further guidance on CPD sampling is provided in the Guidance Note on CPD Sampling, and additional guidance on removal from the Register is also available.</p>", "text": "The nature, purpose and value of CPD for practising engineers, together with further information to support Licensees in meeting these requirements, are set out in the CPD Policy Statement. Further guidance on CPD sampling is provided in the Guidance Note on CPD Sampling, and additional guidance on removal from the Register is also available.", "refs": [], "status": "amended"}, {"type": "clause", "id": "CPD-001", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 67-68", "v44Paras": [67, 68], "num": "11.1", "html": "<p>Licensees must establish and implement appropriate policies and practices to support registrants in maintaining and enhancing their professional competence through continuing professional development (CPD). In particular, Licensees must:</p><ul><li>establish and periodically review an appropriate CPD policy;</li><li>promote to registrants and employers the purpose, importance and benefits of CPD;</li><li>encourage access to development opportunities that meet the needs of registrants;</li><li>provide guidance and support to help registrants gain value from their CPD activities; and</li><li>provide registrants with a system that they may use for planning, recording, reviewing and sharing their CPD.</li></ul>", "text": "Licensees must establish and implement appropriate policies and practices to support registrants in maintaining and enhancing their professional competence through continuing professional development (CPD). In particular, Licensees must: establish and periodically review an appropriate CPD policy; promote to registrants and employers the purpose, importance and benefits of CPD; encourage access to development opportunities that meet the needs of registrants; provide guidance and support to help registrants gain value from their CPD activities; and provide registrants with a system that they may use for planning, recording, reviewing and sharing their CPD.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2, "may": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "CPD-002", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 para 69", "v44Paras": [69], "num": "11.2", "html": "<p>Licensees must review a representative sample of not less than 5% of their Engineering Council registrants’ CPD records annually, and must provide appropriate feedback to those sampled.</p>", "text": "Licensees must review a representative sample of not less than 5% of their Engineering Council registrants’ CPD records annually, and must provide appropriate feedback to those sampled.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 2}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "CPD-003", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 69, 70", "v44Paras": [69, 70], "num": "11.3", "html": "<p>Licensees must have a process in place for considering requests for exemption from the CPD review where registrants were not professionally active during the period covered by the sample, or can demonstrate extenuating circumstances that prevented them from maintaining their CPD records. Decisions to grant an exemption rest with the Licensee.</p>", "text": "Licensees must have a process in place for considering requests for exemption from the CPD review where registrants were not professionally active during the period covered by the sample, or can demonstrate extenuating circumstances that prevented them from maintaining their CPD records. Decisions to grant an exemption rest with the Licensee.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "CPD-004", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 69, 70", "v44Paras": [69, 70], "num": "11.4", "html": "<p>Licensees must have arrangements in place to allow registrants who maintain a CPD record for their employer or another body to submit that record for review. The submission of a CPD record maintained elsewhere does not provide grounds for exemption from the Licensee’s CPD review.</p>", "text": "Licensees must have arrangements in place to allow registrants who maintain a CPD record for their employer or another body to submit that record for review. The submission of a CPD record maintained elsewhere does not provide grounds for exemption from the Licensee’s CPD review.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}, {"type": "clause", "id": "CPD-005", "kind": "regulation", "v44": "v4.4 paras 69, 70", "v44Paras": [69, 70], "num": "11.5", "html": "<p>Licensees must adopt and implement a policy and process for the removal from the Register of individuals who persistently fail to respond to, or engage with, requests to provide a CPD record.</p>", "text": "Licensees must adopt and implement a policy and process for the removal from the Register of individuals who persistently fail to respond to, or engage with, requests to provide a CPD record.", "refs": [], "status": "amended", "modality": {"must": 1}, "tables": []}], "subsections": [], "num": 11, "id": "S11", "anchor": "continuing-professional-development", "licence": "registration"}], "index": {"DEF-001": "1.1", "DEF-002": "1.2", "V5-074": "1.3", "DEF-003": "1.4", "DEF-004": "1.5", "V5-075": "1.6", "DEF-005": "1.7", "DEF-006": "1.8", "DEF-007": "1.9", "DEF-008": "1.10", "DEF-009": "1.11", "DEF-010": "1.12", "DEF-011": "1.13", "V5-003": "2.1", "V5-005": "2.2", "V5-007": "2.3", "V5-012": "3.1", "V5-014": "3.2", "V5-009": "3.3", "V5-017": "4.1", "V5-018": "4.2", "V5-076": "4.3", "UKU-016": "5.1", "UKU-007": "5.2", "UKU-003": "5.3", "UKU-001": "5.4", "UKU-004": "5.5", "UKU-006": "5.6", "V5-071": "5.7", "UKU-008": "5.8", "UKU-009": "5.9", "UKU-017": "5.10", "UKU-018": "5.11", "UKU-014": "5.12", "UKU-011": "5.13", "UKU-012": "5.14", "V5-019": "6.1", "V5-020": "6.2", "V5-023": "6.3", "V5-077": "6.4", "V5-078": "6.5", "V5-072": "6.6", "V5-022": "6.7", "V5-024": "6.8", "V5-025": "7.1", "V5-026": "7.2", "V5-027": "8.1", "V5-035": "9.1", "V5-036": "9.2", "V5-042": "9.3", "V5-043": "9.4", "V5-044": "9.5", "V5-045": "9.6", "V5-046": "9.7", "V5-047": "9.8", "V5-079": "9.9", "V5-048": "9.10", "V5-049": "9.11", "V5-080": "9.12", "V5-050": "9.13", "V5-052": "9.14", "V5-051": "9.15", "V5-053": "9.16", "V5-054": "9.17", "V5-055": "9.18", "V5-040": "9.19", "V5-059": "9.20", "V5-056": "9.21", "IPD-001": "10.1", "IPD-005": "10.2", "IPD-006": "10.3", "IPD-007": "10.4", "IPD-008": "10.5", "IPD-010": "10.6", "IPD-013": "10.7", "IPD-009": "10.8", "IPD-011": "10.9", "IPD-012": "10.10", "CPD-001": "11.1", "CPD-002": "11.2", "CPD-003": "11.3", "CPD-004": "11.4", "CPD-005": "11.5"}}, "definitions": {"note": "Regulatory definitions, generated from the DEF clauses in content.json. Do not edit here; edit the master draft and regenerate. Match longest term first, so \"peer review\" wins over \"peer\".", "definitions": [{"id": "DEF-001", "num": "1.1", "term": "peer", "variants": ["peers"], "html": "<p>A <em>peer</em> is a professionally registered individual whose experience and training enable them to make an informed professional judgment about the professional review, programme recognition, or other matters.</p>", "text": "A peer is a professionally registered individual whose experience and training enable them to make an informed professional judgment about the professional review, programme recognition, or other matters."}, {"id": "DEF-002", "num": "1.2", "term": "peer review", "variants": ["peer reviews"], "html": "<p>A <em>peer review</em> is an assessment in which one or more peers consider appropriate evidence against defined requirements and make a documented professional judgment.</p>", "text": "A peer review is an assessment in which one or more peers consider appropriate evidence against defined requirements and make a documented professional judgment."}, {"id": "DEF-003", "num": "1.4", "term": "committee responsible for registration", "variants": [], "html": "<p>The <em>committee responsible for registration</em> is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its registration activity and make or authorise decisions on applications for registration.</p>", "text": "The committee responsible for registration is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its registration activity and make or authorise decisions on applications for registration."}, {"id": "DEF-004", "num": "1.5", "term": "committee responsible for recognition", "variants": [], "html": "<p>A <em>committee responsible for recognition</em> is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its programme-recognition activity and its accreditation of IPD schemes, and to make or authorise decisions on the recognition of programmes and the accreditation of schemes. This may be one and the same as the committee responsible for registration.</p>", "text": "A committee responsible for recognition is the body appointed by a Licensee to oversee its programme-recognition activity and its accreditation of IPD schemes, and to make or authorise decisions on the recognition of programmes and the accreditation of schemes. This may be one and the same as the committee responsible for registration."}, {"id": "DEF-005", "num": "1.7", "term": "recognition", "variants": ["Recognition"], "html": "<p><em>Recognition</em> is the umbrella term for approval and accreditation.</p>", "text": "Recognition is the umbrella term for approval and accreditation."}, {"id": "DEF-006", "num": "1.8", "term": "approval", "variants": ["Approval"], "html": "<p>Approval of a programme of learning attests to its content: its overall design, depth and range of coverage, and the validity and reliability of the assessment of the learner.</p>", "text": "Approval of a programme of learning attests to its content: its overall design, depth and range of coverage, and the validity and reliability of the assessment of the learner."}, {"id": "DEF-007", "num": "1.9", "term": "accreditation", "variants": ["Accreditation"], "html": "<p>Accreditation confirms that the delivery of a programme of learning, or of an IPD scheme, is recognised at a specific site or sites, or at a specific provider. Accreditation incorporates approval: if delivery is accredited, approval of the underlying content is implicit.</p>", "text": "Accreditation confirms that the delivery of a programme of learning, or of an IPD scheme, is recognised at a specific site or sites, or at a specific provider. Accreditation incorporates approval: if delivery is accredited, approval of the underlying content is implicit."}, {"id": "DEF-008", "num": "1.10", "term": "joint board", "variants": ["joint boards", "joint board arrangement"], "html": "<p>A <em>joint board</em> is a single decision-making body established by two or more Licensees to recognise programmes on their behalf, such as the Joint Board of Moderators.</p>", "text": "A joint board is a single decision-making body established by two or more Licensees to recognise programmes on their behalf, such as the Joint Board of Moderators."}, {"id": "DEF-009", "num": "1.11", "term": "joint visit", "variants": ["joint visits"], "html": "<p>A <em>joint visit</em> is an accreditation visit carried out by two or more Licensees together, each of which makes its own recognition decision.</p>", "text": "A joint visit is an accreditation visit carried out by two or more Licensees together, each of which makes its own recognition decision."}, {"id": "DEF-010", "num": "1.12", "term": "interim registrant", "variants": ["interim registrants", "interim registration", "Interim registration"], "html": "<p>An interim registrant is a person whose underpinning knowledge and understanding have been assessed as meeting the Standard, but who has not yet demonstrated the necessary competence for final-stage registration.</p>", "text": "An interim registrant is a person whose underpinning knowledge and understanding have been assessed as meeting the Standard, but who has not yet demonstrated the necessary competence for final-stage registration."}, {"id": "DEF-011", "num": "1.13", "term": "final-stage registrant", "variants": ["final-stage registrants"], "html": "<p>A final-stage registrant is a person who has successfully completed the Professional Review and holds registered status that is not interim.</p>", "text": "A final-stage registrant is a person who has successfully completed the Professional Review and holds registered status that is not interim."}]}, "glossary": {"note": "Helpful terms: NOT regulation. Shown with a distinct style and a \"not a regulatory definition\" tag. Drafted for the prototype except where the source says otherwise. Regulatory definitions live in definitions.json and win on any clash. Editable: the app re-renders from this file.", "terms": [{"term": "Licensee", "variants": ["Licensees", "Licensee's", "Licensees’", "Licensee’s"], "definition": "An organisation licensed by the Engineering Council Board to assess individuals for professional registration. Licensees may also be licensed to recognise programmes of learning and to accredit IPD schemes. Sometimes known informally as Professional Engineering Institutions, or PEIs.", "source": "RfR front matter (Welcome)"}, {"term": "Engineering Council", "variants": [], "definition": "The UK regulatory body for the engineering profession, operating under Royal Charter. It sets the standards for professional registration, licenses Licensees and holds the national Register.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "the Register", "variants": ["Register"], "definition": "The national register of professionally registered engineers and technicians held by the Engineering Council. Its categories are CEng, IEng, EngTech and ICTTech, each with final-stage and interim sections.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "registrant", "variants": ["registrants", "Engineering Council registrants", "Engineering Council registrant"], "definition": "An individual holding registration with the Engineering Council in any category. See also the defined terms interim registrant and final-stage registrant.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "underpinning knowledge and understanding", "variants": ["UKU"], "definition": "The knowledge and understanding an applicant needs to underpin competent professional practice in the category sought. Assessed before, or alongside, the Professional Review, against the Knowledge Benchmark.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Knowledge Benchmark", "variants": ["Knowledge Benchmarks"], "definition": "The Engineering Council reference against which Licensees decide whether an applicant’s underpinning knowledge and understanding meet the requirement. Its final name and publication date depend on the Knowledge Statement project.", "source": "drafted; pending the Knowledge Statement project (M5 asked for a fuller description)"}, {"term": "initial assessment", "variants": [], "definition": "A clerical check that an application is complete and the evidence submitted is genuine. It is not peer review; it makes the application ready for peer review.", "source": "RfR section 4 explanatory text"}, {"term": "Professional Review", "variants": [], "definition": "The peer assessment of an applicant’s competence and commitment. It has two components, a review of documentary evidence and an interview, and Licensees may add others.", "source": "drafted from section 6"}, {"term": "competence and commitment", "variants": [], "definition": "What an applicant must demonstrate for registration, as set out for each category in UK-SPEC or the ICTTech Standard.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "UK-SPEC", "variants": [], "definition": "The UK Standard for Professional Engineering Competence and Commitment: the Prescribed Standard for CEng, IEng and EngTech registration.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "ICTTech Standard", "variants": ["ICT*Tech* Standard"], "definition": "The Information and Communications Technology Technician Standard: the Prescribed Standard for ICTTech registration.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Prescribed Standards", "variants": ["Prescribed Standard"], "definition": "Standards owned by the Engineering Council that set the competence and commitment required for each category of registration: UK-SPEC and the ICTTech Standard. The term comes from the Code of Practice on Document Hierarchy.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Recognised Standards", "variants": ["Recognised Standard"], "definition": "The collective name for Proprietary Standards and Derived Standards (see Table 2).", "source": "RfR section 3 explanatory text"}, {"term": "Proprietary Standard", "variants": ["Proprietary Standards"], "definition": "A standard developed by the Engineering Council with Licensees, based on and fully meeting UK-SPEC, such as a sector-specific or specialist standard.", "source": "Table 2"}, {"term": "Derived Standard", "variants": ["Derived Standards"], "definition": "A Licensee’s own standard, based on and fully meeting UK-SPEC or a Proprietary Standard, authorised by QAC.", "source": "Table 2"}, {"term": "AHEP", "variants": ["Accreditation of Higher Education Programmes"], "definition": "Accreditation of Higher Education Programmes: the learning outcomes for accredited higher education programmes.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "AAQA", "variants": ["Approval and Accreditation of Qualifications and Apprenticeships"], "definition": "Approval and Accreditation of Qualifications and Apprenticeships: the learning outcomes and competence framework for approved and accredited qualifications and apprenticeships.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Registration Standards Committee", "variants": ["RSC"], "definition": "The Engineering Council committee responsible for registration standards. It recommends changes to these Regulations and authorises certain arrangements under them.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Quality Assurance Committee", "variants": ["QAC"], "definition": "The Engineering Council committee that audits Licensees’ application of these Regulations, including through licence review, and authorises Derived Standards.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Regulations for Licensing", "variants": ["RfL"], "definition": "The Engineering Council’s regulations governing the licensing of Licensees: a sibling document to these Regulations.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "Regulations Explanatory Notes", "variants": [], "definition": "Engineering Council publications that Licensees must follow, explaining how a regulation is to be applied. Their standing is set by the Code of Practice on Document Hierarchy.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "CEng", "variants": ["Chartered Engineer"], "definition": "Chartered Engineer: a category of the Register.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "IEng", "variants": ["Incorporated Engineer"], "definition": "Incorporated Engineer: a category of the Register.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "EngTech", "variants": ["Engineering Technician"], "definition": "Engineering Technician: a category of the Register.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "ICTTech", "variants": ["ICT*Tech*", "ICT Technician"], "definition": "Information and Communications Technology Technician: a category of the Register.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "IPD", "variants": ["Initial Professional Development"], "definition": "Initial Professional Development: the development of competence and commitment that prepares an engineer or technician for Professional Review.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "CPD", "variants": ["continuing professional development", "Continuing Professional Development"], "definition": "Continuing Professional Development: the maintenance and enhancement of professional competence throughout a registrant’s career.", "source": "drafted"}, {"term": "technical report", "variants": ["technical reports"], "definition": "A report an applicant may be asked to submit to demonstrate underpinning knowledge and understanding, assessed by peer review and, for IEng and CEng, followed by a technical report interview.", "source": "drafted from UKU-017, UKU-018"}, {"term": "recognised programmes database", "variants": [], "definition": "The Engineering Council’s public listing of approved and accredited programmes (the course search).", "source": "drafted"}]}, "overlays": {"note": "Guidance shown in place, in an overlay on the regulation it supports. Extracts are VERBATIM from current Guidance Notes, Policy Statements (kind: policy) and the Engineering Council governance Regulations (kind: regulations), so they are real text, not drafted. status planned = placeholder for guidance not yet written. Show the source document, version and location on every overlay. Edit tools/gn_overlays_src.json, not this file.", "overlays": [{"id": "GN-RS-4", "target": "V5-012", "trigger": "Guidance Note on Recognised Standards", "title": "What “fully meeting UK-SPEC” means", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Recognised Standards", "version": "Version 1, approved by RSC 12 May 2022", "location": "Section 4: Fully/partially meeting UK-SPEC", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Recognised Standards.pdf"}, "extract": [{"ref": "§4", "text": "Derived and Proprietary Standards must fully meet UK-SPEC. This means that every competence statement in the applicable UK-SPEC category (CEng, IEng, EngTech) must be met or exceeded by a competence statement in the Derived or Proprietary Standard."}], "caveat": "The Guidance Note quotes RfR v4.4 paragraph 12, which V5-012 replaces. The Recognised Standards Framework is an annex to this Guidance Note."}, {"id": "GN-INTL-WA", "target": "V5-071", "trigger": null, "title": "Applying the Washington Accord (CEng)", "source": {"document": "Using international education agreements: the Accords, EUR-ACE and the ENGINEERS EUROPE EEED", "version": "v3.7, February 2025", "location": "Washington Accord", "file": "07 Reference/Using international engineering education agreements Accords EUR-ACE EEED_.pdf"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Before full membership", "text": "Programmes accredited by a signatory prior to the date of its admission to full membership of the Accord may be recognised at the discretion of the assessing PEI and if successful the applicant should be registered as ‘Individual Route’."}, {"ref": "Treatment for CEng", "text": "For the purposes of Chartered Engineer registration programmes recognised under the Washington Accord should be treated in the same way as an ‘Engineering Council CEng accredited’ programmes, subject to the following:", "items": ["Graduates of Washington Accord programmes with intake dates up to and including 30/06/2015 may be treated as meeting the educational requirements and classified as Standard Route for CEng registration.", "Graduates of Washington Accord programmes with an intake date of 1 July 2015 or later should be assessed for learning to European Master’s level and classified as Standard or Individual route, depending on any identified further learning requirement and, where applicable, whether this is deemed to have been met through an accredited or approved programme of further learning."]}, {"ref": "Outside national boundaries", "text": "Programmes accredited by Accord signatories outside their national or territorial boundaries should be assessed by PEIs in accordance with their normal procedures and recorded as ‘Individual Route’."}], "caveat": "Quoted verbatim, including the source’s own grammar. The note covers the Dublin and Sydney Accords and EUR-ACE in parallel sections; a full build would offer each."}, {"id": "REG-6C", "target": "V5-007", "kind": "regulations", "trigger": null, "title": "What Guidance Notes are, and how much weight they carry", "source": {"document": "Engineering Council Regulations", "version": "v16, approved by the Board June 2025", "file": "07 Reference/engc-regulations-v16-june-2025.pdf", "location": "Regulation 6C: Engineering Council Guidance, paras 1 and 2"}, "extract": [{"ref": "6C.1", "text": "Guidance Notes are published by the Engineering Council’s committees under the powers delegated by the Board. Guidance Notes produced by the panels may require Board approval before publication unless explicit delegation has already been given to do so. Such Guidance Notes should comprise:", "items": ["a. Documents produced under an express authority in Regulations, e.g. the Regulations for Licensing. Unless otherwise stated, these carry the same authority as the parent Regulation;", "b. External Directives and other documents reproduced for convenience, particularly in the International area. These have varying degrees of authority, normally apparent from the text;", "c. Guidance Notes, produced by Engineering Council committees and panels which are endorsed by the Board. Guidance Notes are complementary to Regulations: in simplistic terms, Regulations are ‘shall’ whereas Guidance Notes are ‘should’."]}, {"ref": "6C.2", "text": "Guidance Notes should reflect a consensus view of best practice across, or in some cases beyond, the profession. Some could be described as firmer than others. During Licensing Reviews, material shortcomings in a Licensee’s procedures or practices compared with relevant guidance could be evidence of a need for improvement."}], "caveat": "Engineering Council governance Regulations, not RfR. Shown here because 2.3 relies on the distinction it draws."}, {"id": "GN-RS-3", "target": "V5-014", "trigger": null, "title": "Requirements a Recognised Standard may add", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Recognised Standards", "version": "Version 1, approved by RSC 12 May 2022", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Recognised Standards.pdf", "location": "Section 3: Renewal, Revalidation, and other requirements"}, "extract": [{"ref": "§3", "text": "Recognised Standards may be subject to requirements additional to those in the Regulations for Registration or other Engineering Council guidance or regulations. For instance, regarding the renewal or revalidation of registration, or requirements for the submission of CPD samples.\n\nLicensees should consider each published Recognised Standard’s requirements the authorititative source. Unless specifically extended by the specific requirements of the Recognised Standard, the Regulations for Registration and attached Engineering Council guidance and regulation continue to apply."}], "caveat": "Quoted verbatim, including the source’s typing slip (“authorititative”). This is the guidance v4.4 para 14 footnoted."}, {"id": "REG-7A", "target": "V5-009", "kind": "regulations", "trigger": null, "title": "Registering through an additional Licensee", "source": {"document": "Engineering Council Regulations", "version": "v16, approved by the Board June 2025", "file": "07 Reference/engc-regulations-v16-june-2025.pdf", "location": "Regulation 7A: Payment of Fees, paras 6 to 8"}, "extract": [{"ref": "7A.6", "text": "Should any Registrants elect to become registered through another Licensee or Professional Affiliate as well as the body to which they pay their Registration Fee then the new Licensee or Professional Affiliate may offer such registration subject to the Registrant paying an appropriate Registration Fee,"}, {"ref": "7A.7", "text": "For both Registrants who elect to change the body to which they pay their Registration Fee and Registrants who elect to become registered through an additional Licensee or Professional Affiliate, the new and/or additional registering body shall then assure itself that the individual meets the Regulations for Registration’s requirements for that category of registration. This shall include a review of the Registrant’s most recent CPD record, the Licensee also having the discretion to request further evidence (e.g. demonstration against specific competences). Such applications shall be subject to the approval of the Licensee’s committee(s) responsible for decisions for registration."}, {"ref": "7A.8", "text": "Where multiple Principal registrations are held by the same individual, additional registration certificates may be applied for but would attract an Additional Certificate Fee, set by the Engineering Council. Each such certificate would be dated as of the date the additional registration was entered on to the Register."}], "caveat": "Engineering Council governance Regulations, not RfR. This governs registration in the same category through a second body; it says nothing about the exceptional circumstances 3.3 mentions."}, {"id": "GN-PR-Q3", "target": "V5-009", "trigger": null, "title": "Holding full registration in more than one section of the Register", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "Assessment, Q3"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q3", "q": "Can an application be considered from an individual who wishes to hold full registered status in more than one section of the Register with the same Licensee?", "text": "Yes, in certain circumstances, and the two applications must be considered individually. Separate guidance is available on this on the Partner Portal."}], "caveat": "The “separate guidance” this answer points to has not been found; colleagues believe none exists. 3.3’s sentence “Guidance about the exceptional circumstances is available” may need to go."}, {"id": "GN-TR-PLANNED", "target": "UKU-017", "status": "planned", "trigger": null, "title": "Guidance on the technical report", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the technical report (planned)", "version": "not yet drafted", "location": "—"}, "extract": [], "caveat": "Placeholder. WG02 agreed that much of the v4.4 technical-report material is guidance rather than regulation and should move to a new Guidance Note. When it exists, its relevant clauses will show here."}, {"id": "GN-PR-Q18", "target": "V5-023", "trigger": null, "title": "Using another language at interview", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "The Professional Review Interview, Q18"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q18", "q": "Can part of the interview be conducted in a language other than English?", "text": "Paragraph 23 of the Regulations for Registration (RfR) applies. Another language may be used purely for the clarification of a word or point, to enable the interview to continue."}], "caveat": "Draft 7 has brought this answer’s second sentence into 6.3 itself, so the guidance and the regulation now say the same thing. One of them can go."}, {"id": "GN-PR-Q15-16", "target": "V5-078", "trigger": null, "title": "Conducting the interview: length and remote interviews", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "The Professional Review Interview, Q15 and Q16"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q15", "q": "What is the ideal length of the interview?", "text": "Ideally between 60 and 90 minutes. Thorough preparation, including assessment of the application and briefing of the assessors, should be completed prior to the interview."}, {"ref": "Q16", "q": "Can the interview be conducted remotely?", "text": "Yes. Applicants should have agreed to this type of interview. Robust arrangements must be made to verify the identity of the applicant, to authenticate any evidence, and to ensure that the interview environment is appropriate and is demonstrably not open to abuse. It is essential that voice and vision contact are maintained throughout the interview. The PRI record should indicate that it was performed remotely.\n\nLicensees should clearly communicate the systems to be used during the interview to the applicant ahead of time, and ensure those systems are robust. Licensees should make clear to applicants whether a remote or in-person interview is the default.\n\nRecords of the how many PRIs are conducted remotely and in person should be maintained. When developing, documenting and approving detailed processes, Licensees should base them on this guidance, specifying the technology employed and procedures to be taken in the event of any break in transmission."}], "caveat": "Updated from v1.3 to v1.4 (October 2023), which renumbered the questions and added guidance on communicating the systems used."}, {"id": "GN-PR-ASSESSORS", "target": "V5-022", "trigger": null, "title": "Choosing and training assessors", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "The Professional Review Interview, Q7, Q10, Q12 and Q13"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q7", "q": "Do the assessors require training?", "text": "Yes, it is essential that the assessors undergo initial training and maintain their competence, and that PRI assessors conduct interviews regularly."}, {"ref": "Q10", "q": "Should the relationship between assessors and applicants be considered?", "text": "Licensees should wherever possible appoint assessors who do not have a working or other relationship with the applicant. When this is impossible, any working or other relationship needs to be made transparent, and there must be appropriate procedures in place for declarations of interest for both parties. Interview assessors should always be from outside the applicant’s immediate organisation."}, {"ref": "Q12", "q": "Is it acceptable to use assessors from other Licensees?", "text": "Yes, providing they met the requirements of paragraph 22 of the Regulations for Registration"}, {"ref": "Q13", "q": "May we use fixed pairings of assessors?", "text": "This is not encouraged. If circumstances require it, then a suitable moderation process should be in place."}], "caveat": "Q12 refers to v4.4 paragraph 22, which 6.7 and 1.3 now carry between them."}, {"id": "GN-PR-REPORT", "target": "V5-024", "trigger": null, "title": "The assessors’ report", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "Reporting, Recommendations and Decisions, Q19 and Q20"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q19", "q": "What is the purpose of the report on the PRI?", "text": "Reports are essential to show the reasons for the assessors’ recommendations and ensure transparency, as well as to facilitate moderation within the Licensee, and to provide evidence of process for Engineering Council licensing. They also provide essential evidence in the case of an appeal by an applicant against a decision, or in the case of any subsequent challenge to the suitability of a successful applicant. Assessors must therefore complete a report on each applicant."}, {"ref": "Q20", "q": "What needs to be in the report?", "text": "The report should record the assessors’ judgements and recommendation, and these should be justified by appropriate evidential statements, that show whether the applicant has demonstrated all of the competence and commitment requirements for the category of registration which is sought. The statements should reflect any evidence which the assessors may have from any assessment of the applicant carried out before the PRI, as well as from the PRI itself. The report should only contain matters relating to the assessment of competence and commitment, and extraneous statements about the applicant should be avoided."}], "caveat": null}, {"id": "GN-PR-DECISION", "target": "V5-025", "trigger": null, "title": "The committee’s decision", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "Reporting, Recommendations and Decisions, Q23; After the interview, Q28 and Q29"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q23", "q": "Do the assessors decide an applicant may be registered?", "text": "No, they make recommendations to the committee responsible for registration."}, {"ref": "Q28", "q": "Can the committee responsible for registration decide not to accept a recommendation?", "text": "Yes, but this should only happen in exceptional circumstances, and only if the committee’s decision is not to accept the assessors’ recommendations to award a title to an individual. The reasons for such a decision should be recorded and there should be a review of the process."}, {"ref": "Q29", "q": "Where the assessors’ recommendation is not to award a title, can a committee decide to reject that decision and award the title (or a different title) to the individual?", "text": "No. This is not a fail-safe decision. The committee, following a review of all the evidence and the interview assessors’ report, has only two options: to accept the recommendation or direct that the individual be offered a second interview.\n\nThe reasons for such a decision should be recorded and there should be a review of processes, especially the decision to send to interview and the assessors’ findings. RfR paragraph 25 states that the Committee “may recommend that the applicant be invited to repeat one or more components of the Professional Review”. However, particular attention should be paid to the possibility of interview assessor bias having played any part in the recommendation not to award a title, and steps taken to ensure a new assessment is not subject to bias."}], "caveat": "Q29 quotes v4.4 paragraph 25."}, {"id": "GN-PR-Q30", "target": "V5-027", "trigger": null, "title": "When an appeal is upheld", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on the Professional Review", "version": "v1.4, October 2023", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-the-professional-review.pdf", "location": "After the interview, Q30 (closing paragraph)"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q30", "q": "Can an unsuccessful applicant make an appeal against the committee’s decision?", "text": "Where an appeal against the process followed is upheld by an Appeals Committee, the Appeals Committee should inform the Committee responsible for registration of the decision, the reason for the decision, and a recommendation on the process to be followed for re-assessment of the elements on the process determined not to be legitimate."}], "caveat": "The rest of the answer quotes v4.4 paragraph 27, which 8.1 carries forward, so only the guidance itself is shown."}, {"id": "GN-AA-F", "target": "V5-044", "trigger": null, "title": "Accrediting where the Licensee has an interest", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation", "version": "v3.4, August 2024", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation Issue 3.4.pdf", "location": "Annex F: Accreditation where Licensees may have an interest"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Annex F", "text": "Licensees may occasionally be asked to accredit programmes in which they or their partner organisations have an interest. To safeguard accreditation standards and avoid any reputational risk arising from perceived conflict of interest Licensees will usually be expected to demonstrate independence from awarding and delivery institutions when accrediting.\n\nOn occasions where a potential conflict of interest exists where a Licensee wishes to accredit a programme that they or a partner organisation have an involvement with, the following conditions must be met:", "items": ["The visit must be conducted with at least one other Licensee.", "Another Licensee must take the lead and appoint the Chair.", "The Chair will be responsible for ensuring no conflict of interest impacts upon the accreditation decision.", "The Chair will be responsible for achieving agreement on the accreditation decision with the Licensee that has the potential conflict of interest.", "If agreement cannot be reached the Chair must refer the matter to RSC."]}], "caveat": "9.5 covers the narrower case where the Licensee is itself the awarding institution; this annex covers any interest."}, {"id": "GN-AA-H", "target": "V5-046", "trigger": null, "title": "Limits on compensation and condonement", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation", "version": "v3.4, August 2024", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation Issue 3.4.pdf", "location": "Annex H: Guidance on Compensation and Condonement (the policy requirements)"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Definitions", "text": "The Engineering Council defines compensation as: “The practice of allowing marginal failure (ie not more than ten percentage points below the nominal pass mark) of one or more modules and awarding credit for them, often on the basis of good overall academic performance.”\n\nThe Engineering Council defines condonement as: “The practice of allowing students to fail one or more module(s) with a fail mark of more than ten percentage points below the nominal pass mark, yet still qualify for the award of the degree.”"}, {"ref": "1", "text": "Evidence that all AHEP 4.0 learning outcomes are met by all variants of each programme must be provided before accreditation can be granted."}, {"ref": "2", "text": "No condonement of modules delivering AHEP 4.0 learning outcomes is allowed."}, {"ref": "3", "text": "A maximum of 30 credits in a Bachelors or integrated Masters degree programme can be compensated, and a maximum of 20 credits in a Masters degree other than the integrated Masters degree."}, {"ref": "4", "text": "Major individual and group-based project modules must not be compensated."}, {"ref": "5", "text": "The minimum module mark for which compensation is allowed is no more than ten percentage points below the nominal module pass mark (or equivalent if a grade-based marking scheme is used)."}], "caveat": "This is v4.4 footnote 7. The numbered requirements are the policy itself, shown in bold in the Guidance Note; the Note adds worked explanation after each."}, {"id": "GN-AA-4", "target": "V5-047", "trigger": null, "title": "Accreditation outcomes, and “partial accreditation”", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation", "version": "v3.4, August 2024", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation Issue 3.4.pdf", "location": "Section 4: Accreditation Decisions"}, "extract": [{"ref": "§4", "text": "Qualifying phrases such as ‘provisional accreditation’ and ‘partial accreditation’ are not used."}, {"ref": "§4", "q": "Are programmes still able to be ‘partially accredited’?", "text": "The term was discontinued some time ago. Programmes may be accredited as partially delivering the knowledge and understanding for a professional title, eg accredited with further learning required or as further learning This means that a programme delivers the learning outcomes required for the accreditation (for example an Honours degree for CEng) but further learning is required to demonstrate all of the knowledge and understanding required for registration."}], "caveat": "Relevant to WG03’s finding that partial recognition is undefined and reads as semi-failure. The Note cites RfR 4.1 paragraph numbers."}, {"id": "PS-RBAV-18", "target": "V5-051", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "Major changes between accreditation visits", "source": {"document": "Risk-based approach to accreditation visits: policy statement", "version": "v1.1, January 2025", "file": "07 Reference/risk-based-accreditation-visits-policy-january-2025.pdf", "location": "Visits to consider major changes to programmes, paras 18 and 20"}, "extract": [{"ref": "18", "text": "PEIs must place a condition on providers to inform them of major changes during the period of accreditation that might affect the delivery of the specified programme outcomes."}, {"ref": "20", "text": "A visit is required to confirm continuation of accreditation to programmes that have undergone a major change in between the normal cycle of accreditation visits, but this may be a light-touch virtual visit (which may be shorter in duration than a full virtual visit) if no more than 50% of the content has changed. In some cases where less than 30% of programme content has changed, the requirement to visit may be waived completely (see information below in paragraphs 25to 3429 on waiving the requirement for an accreditation visit)."}], "caveat": "Quoted verbatim, including the source’s cross-reference slip in para 20 (“paragraphs 25to 3429”)."}, {"id": "PS-RBAV-11", "target": "V5-040", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "When a visit must have a significant in-person element", "source": {"document": "Risk-based approach to accreditation visits: policy statement", "version": "v1.1, January 2025", "file": "07 Reference/risk-based-accreditation-visits-policy-january-2025.pdf", "location": "Visits where a significant in-person element is mandatory, para 11"}, "extract": [{"ref": "11", "text": "A visit containing a significant in-person element is mandatory where one or more of the following apply:", "items": ["a. The programme is new to accreditation and is substantially different (eg a different discipline without commonality) to previous programmes currently accredited at the provider.", "b. The previous Action Plan contained a requirement relating to any inadequacy of physical or staff resources which has not since been confirmed as addressed during an on-site monitoring visit.", "c. A recent visit report indicated issues/concerns in properly monitoring laboratory/workshop provision, with regards to health and safety, or identified from meeting with students, which has not since been confirmed as addressed during an on-site monitoring visit.", "d. An education regulator has raised concerns related to the (overall or engineering) education provided by the provider. PEIs may request details of any relevant audits or other assessments as part of submission documentation.", "e. The provider/department/location (including any franchise or partnership provision being considered for accreditation) has not received an in-person or hybrid accreditation visit previously.", "f. The last full accreditation visit to the provider/department/location did not involve a significant in-person element.", "g. The submission mentions changes (whether to programmes or within the provider - for example to staffing, physical facilities, assessment processes, management of the programme(s) or regulations) that the PEI judges to warrant closer scrutiny through an in-person or hybrid visit.", "h. The PEI feels that an in-person or hybrid visit is appropriate for another reason."]}, {"ref": "11", "text": "Where none of a-h above apply, the visit requirement may be fulfilled through an in-person, hybrid or virtual visit at the discretion of the PEI(s)."}], "caveat": "This is v4.4 footnote 6. Replaces the Annex K summary used in the first build: this is the policy itself. Footnote 2 (on which education regulator) removed from item d."}, {"id": "GN-AA-6", "target": "V5-040", "trigger": null, "title": "Programmes delivered on more than one campus", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation", "version": "v3.4, August 2024", "file": "07 Reference/Guidance Note on Academic Accreditation Issue 3.4.pdf", "location": "Section 6: Location of Study"}, "extract": [{"ref": "§6", "text": "Licensee(s) must normally visit all campuses involved in delivery of programmes they are invited to accredit, or only accredit for delivery in campuses visited. A visit is usually required to enable Licensed Members to consider evidence from a range of indicators, including those listed in regulation 44 of the RfR 4.1. If a programme is delivered on multiple campuses (including through franchise or partnership arrangements) students will only be considered to have completed an accredited programme if they have completed the programme at a campus for which accreditation is confirmed. In some circumstances a visit may be hybrid or virtual (the policy on risk-based accreditation provides more information)"}, {"ref": "§6", "text": "Licensees should record on the courses database any limit to the scope of accreditation awarded."}], "caveat": "The Note cites regulation 44 of RfR 4.1. Footnote markers on “partnership” and “campus” removed; the footnotes define partnership delivery and what completing at a campus means."}, {"id": "PS-RBAV-27", "target": "V5-059", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "Visiting every provider", "source": {"document": "Risk-based approach to accreditation visits: policy statement", "version": "v1.1, January 2025", "file": "07 Reference/risk-based-accreditation-visits-policy-january-2025.pdf", "location": "Waiving the requirement for an accreditation visit, paras 27 and 29"}, "extract": [{"ref": "27", "text": "Not undertaking an accreditation visit is expected to be the exception rather than the norm. The default position is therefore that each provider involved in the delivery of a programme will be visited (whether in-person, virtually or through a hybrid visit)."}, {"ref": "29", "text": "For the purposes of such considerations, the term ‘provider’ includes the delivery provider (which could be for example a university, college, or employer) and any person or organisation external to the delivery provider with a lead responsibility for a significant element of the programme. The awarding organisation may be the same as the provider (as is typical with university degrees) or may be an organisation that awards a qualification delivered by multiple providers."}], "caveat": null}, {"id": "PS-RBAV-26", "target": "V5-056", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "Conditions for waiving an accreditation visit", "source": {"document": "Risk-based approach to accreditation visits: policy statement", "version": "v1.1, January 2025", "file": "07 Reference/risk-based-accreditation-visits-policy-january-2025.pdf", "location": "Waiving the requirement for an accreditation visit, para 26"}, "extract": [{"ref": "26", "text": "A PEI may waive the requirement for an accreditation visit where the programme concerned has significant commonality with programmes already accredited by the PEI, and provided that sufficient evidence is available, including to demonstrate that factors which can normally only be reviewed during a visit are satisfactory.", "items": ["The process must ensure that high standards of accreditation are maintained.", "Programmes must comply with a current edition of AHEP or AAQA.", "The requirement for a visit must not be waived if there is more than a maximum 30% difference in content from a programme accredited at the last accreditation visit.", "A visit must not be waived if major changes have occurred with regards to department or provider resources or governance since the most recent accreditation visit.", "The provider must provide a good reason for requesting accreditation between the normal schedule of visits rather than awaiting the next round of accreditation.", "After reviewing evidence, the PEI may still require a visit before awarding accreditation."]}], "caveat": "Drafting point: 9.21 says these conditions “are set out in AHEP and AAQA”. They are set out here, in the risk-based visits policy."}, {"id": "PS-IPD-1", "target": "IPD-007", "kind": "policy", "trigger": "IPD Policy Statement", "title": "What an accredited IPD scheme offers", "source": {"document": "Initial Professional Development Policy Statement", "version": "Issue 1, approved by RSC 14 February 2019", "file": "07 Reference/ipd-policy-statement-2019.pdf", "location": "Accreditation of structured IPD schemes"}, "extract": [{"ref": "IPD", "text": "IPD schemes differ greatly, as each is designed to meet the needs of the employer. However, all accredited IPD schemes will have clear objectives which satisfy the standards of competence and commitment set out in UK-SPEC and the ICTTech standard. Accredited IPD must provide assessment and certification of achievement, and have the support of senior and line management. The value of accredited IPD is in how it offers individuals opportunities to develop towards professional registration and encourages recording of evidence for Professional Review."}], "caveat": "This is v4.4 footnote 8. The Policy Statement predates AHEP 4 and the 2020 RfR; it is due for review."}, {"id": "PS-IPD-SITES", "target": "IPD-013", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "Schemes delivered at more than one site", "source": {"document": "Initial Professional Development Policy Statement", "version": "Issue 1, approved by RSC 14 February 2019", "file": "07 Reference/ipd-policy-statement-2019.pdf", "location": "Accreditation of structured IPD schemes"}, "extract": [{"ref": "IPD", "text": "Accreditation assessors visit one or more locations where the scheme is delivered or administered. Where the same scheme is delivered at multiple sites, copies of examples of assessment and certification materials shall be examined as part of the process. Locations visited should be representative of an individual’s experience. Conversations with assessors and candidates should form part of the accreditation process; these may be conducted remotely if the scheme is delivered at multiple sites. The employer should demonstrate that systems are in place to ensure individuals receive consistent support if they are moving between sites."}], "caveat": null}, {"id": "PS-IPD-JOINT", "target": "IPD-012", "kind": "policy", "trigger": null, "title": "Joint visits, delegation and exclusivity", "source": {"document": "Initial Professional Development Policy Statement", "version": "Issue 1, approved by RSC 14 February 2019", "file": "07 Reference/ipd-policy-statement-2019.pdf", "location": "Accreditation of structured IPD schemes"}, "extract": [{"ref": "IPD", "text": "Joint visits are appropriate where an employer is seeking accreditation from more than one Licensed Member. However, accreditation shall not be jointly granted; each Licensed Member shall make a decision to grant or renew accreditation on its own.\n\nThe duration of accreditation is decided by the Licensed Member but shall be no longer than five years. Where re-accreditation is undertaken jointly, the participating PEIs may agree to delegate the visit among themselves. However, PEIs delegating visits must ensure they remotely obtain evidence from candidates each time through online or paper-based communications. A visit to re-accredit a particular IPD scheme may not be delegated to another PEI twice successively."}, {"ref": "IPD", "text": "Accreditation of IPD schemes may not be an exclusive agreement, and must not preclude the employer or its employees from working with other PEIs for implementation and assessment of IPD."}], "caveat": "The delegation rule (no successive delegated visits) and the exclusivity rule have no counterpart in Draft 7’s section 10."}, {"id": "PS-CPD-PLAN", "target": "CPD-001", "kind": "policy", "trigger": "CPD Policy Statement", "title": "Planning, recording and reflecting on CPD", "source": {"document": "CPD Policy Statement", "version": "v3.1, February 2025", "file": "07 Reference/cpd-policy-statement-31.pdf", "location": "pages 1 and 2"}, "extract": [{"ref": "CPD", "text": "Individual registrants are best placed to determine their needs and how to meet them and should set this out in a CPD Plan, which should be reviewed on a regular basis, and at least once a year. Often, employers or experienced colleagues will play a significant part in the development and execution of an individual’s CPD; but it is the registrant’s responsibility to proactively identify their professional development needs and opportunities."}, {"ref": "CPD", "text": "Whatever its purpose or nature, learning through CPD should be reflective and should relate to specific objectives even if these are only to maintain their professional engineering competence. Having a regularly reviewed development plan will facilitate learning, although there will always be a place for unplanned activities. Registrants should record both their CPD activities and what they have learned or achieved through them, and relate this to any planned objectives. UK-SPEC and the ICTTech standard may serve as a framework for setting personal development objectives. Registrants may also find value in planning learning against contextualised or mutually recognised competence frameworks such as the Global Responsibility Competency Compass. Doing this will help them to determine their future needs and plan accordingly, as part of a cyclical process. It will also encourage an outcome-based approach which is more appropriate to professional learning than relying solely on input measures such as hours or points."}], "caveat": "This is v4.4 footnote 9, and the home of R03 (reflection; UK-SPEC as a planning scaffold). Footnote marker removed from “Compass”."}, {"id": "GN-CPDS-SAMPLE", "target": "CPD-002", "trigger": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "title": "Drawing the sample, and who reviews it", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "version": "current Partner Portal issue (undated)", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-cpd-sampling.pdf", "location": "Q2, Q11 and Q12"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q2", "q": "Who should be part of a sample?", "text": "CPD sample selections must draw from all professionally active Engineering Council registrants. While samples are never fully ‘random’, Licensed PEIs should operate on the principle that no one registrant should be significantly more likely to be sampled than any other.\n\nLicensed PEIs may decide that individuals who have submitted records one year are not asked to do so again the following year/years, with a view to sampling a broad range of registrants and registrant grades.\n\nLicensed PEIs may extend CPD sampling to non-registrants and general members, but only sampling of Engineering Council registrants should be reported in the Annual Licence Reports and at licence review."}, {"ref": "Q11", "q": "Who should review records submitted by registrants?", "text": "Peer review is the key principle. Registrants’ records should be reviewed by an assessment process that is overseen by trained registrants, and may be supported by systems and staff. Licensed PEIs should establish a pool of suitably training and qualified registrants from which reviewers can be drawn. CPD reviewers should be trained before carrying out reviews and at intervals thereafter, in line with the Licensee’s training policies. Feedback and support provided must be appropriate to the registration grade of the individual.\n\nUse of staff, or of automated systems, may be appropriate in offering feedback to registrants. Licensed PEIs should operate on the principle that where feedback is positive – that is, where the CPD record presented clearly demonstrates reflection and relates to objectives – staff or automated systems can provide the feedback. However, where the CPD record is deficient in some aspect, and the registrant requires guidance, feedback should be produced or approved by another registrant."}, {"ref": "Q12", "q": "What should the sample size be?", "text": "From January 2026, Licensed PEIs are required to sample at least 5% of their Engineering Council registrants each year. This includes those on Registers based on Recognised Standards, unless otherwise specified in the Recognised Standard.\n\nIn line with the aspiration of adding value to professional registration, where Licensed PEIs are able to extend their CPD sample to more than 5%, Registration Standards Committee has recommended that they are encouraged to do so."}], "caveat": "This is v4.4 footnote 11. Footnote marker removed from “Recognised Standards”."}, {"id": "GN-CPDS-EXEMPT", "target": "CPD-003", "trigger": null, "title": "Who is professionally active, and exemptions", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "version": "current Partner Portal issue (undated)", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-cpd-sampling.pdf", "location": "Q13 and Q14"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q13", "q": "What is meant by “professionally active”?", "text": "The Policy Statement says that those who are not professionally active may request exemption from a CPD sample. Registrants who are temporarily inactive should consider maintaining a level of CPD commensurate with their return to professional practice.\n\nThose who are not employed but engaged in voluntary activity, whether for their Licensed PEI, the Engineering Council, or a comparable body (for example within the governance structure of their Licensed PEI) or in activity such as professional reviewing or accreditation of academic programmes and professional development schemes are considered to be professionally active. Such volunteers should be encouraged to record and reflect on their practice and learning, and should not be granted exemption from a CPD sample on those grounds.\n\nGenerally, it is for individuals to declare themselves as exempt against their Licensed PEIs’ published criteria, though the Licensed PEI’s committee responsible for CPD sampling should have the final say on whether a registrant should be considered professionally active, based on this guidance."}, {"ref": "Q14", "q": "In what circumstances can a registrant request exemption from a sample?", "text": "Licensed PEIs should seek to balance a duty of care to registrants’ personal wellbeing with the need to maintain professional standards. Decisions on whether to grant exemptions from a CPD sample – and the duration of such exemptions – should be overseen by the Licensed PEI’s committee responsible for CPD sampling, and should be tracked and recorded.\n\nThe size of the initial sample taken – before exemptions are requested or granted – should be the figure reported in the Annual Licence Report."}], "caveat": null}, {"id": "GN-CPDS-RECORDS", "target": "CPD-004", "trigger": null, "title": "Accepting records kept for other purposes", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "version": "current Partner Portal issue (undated)", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-cpd-sampling.pdf", "location": "Q4"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q4", "q": "Does that mean we can accept records prepared for other purposes?", "text": "Yes; provided that they include the necessary information.\n\nRecords prepared for other professional bodies or regulators, or development logs maintained for employers, should be acceptable. Reflection by registrants on the value of the CPD they have undertaken is an essential element. Where records for employers do not give an adequate account of reflection, registrants should be asked to provide an appropriate supplemental record of reflection."}], "caveat": null}, {"id": "GN-CPDS-REMOVAL", "target": "CPD-005", "trigger": "guidance on removal from the Register", "title": "Registrants who do not respond", "source": {"document": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "version": "current Partner Portal issue (undated)", "file": "07 Reference/guidance-note-on-cpd-sampling.pdf", "location": "Q18"}, "extract": [{"ref": "Q18", "q": "What should be done if registrants do not respond to a request for CPD records?", "text": "When registrants who are invited to submit CPD records do not respond, or respond negatively, the Licensed PEI’s first action may be to contact the individual to explain the CPD sampling process and its purpose. Previous experience suggests that most non-respondents will at this point agree to submit a record.\n\nEngineering Council regulations mandate removal from the Register of individuals who persistently do not respond to or engage with requests for CPD records from a Licensed PEI. There is no call to remove an individual from the register for submitting an unsatisfactory CPD record. Where submissions do not show evidence of appropriate CPD or reflection, support from the Licensed PEI would be offered rather than any sanction.\n\nShould a registrant be removed from the Register for this reason, Licensed PEIs should ensure the removal code used is ‘CPD’.\n\nThe concept of natural justice should be applied to Licensed PEIs’ efforts to communicate with those who do not engage with CPD sampling. It is for Licensed PEIs to define what is deemed persistent in accordance with their own policies and procedures, but this should be based on the Licensed PEIs making reasonable endeavours to communicate with sampled individuals.\n\nNon-compliance can be significantly cut down by notifying sampled registrants of the ‘intention to sample’ ahead of the formal request. The intention is to give registrants ample time to prepare submissions – and to give ample time for communications with reticent registrants.\n\nLicensed PEIs should provide to the Engineering Council a list of registrants removed from the Register for non-compliance with a CPD sample each year. This information will be made available to other Licensed PEIs."}], "caveat": "This appears to be the “additional guidance on removal from the Register” that v4.4 para 70 and the section 11 introduction mention without naming."}]}, "links": {"note": "External-link inventory carried from rfr0.1 (verified 2026-07-24) plus Draft 7 additions. url null = render as a stub with a \"Would link to\" tooltip. Longest match first; first occurrence per block. Do not link inside xrefs, glossary or definition triggers.", "links": [{"match": "UK-SPEC", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/setting-standards/professional-engineering-competence-and-commitment/", "verified": true, "note": "Verified 2026-07-24 (rfr0.1 inventory)"}, {"match": "ICTTech Standard", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/setting-standards/professional-engineering-competence-and-commitment/icttech-standard/", "verified": true, "note": "Verified 2026-07-24"}, {"match": "Recognised Standards", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/setting-standards/professional-engineering-competence-and-commitment/recognised-standards/", "verified": true, "note": "Verified 2026-07-24"}, {"match": "AHEP", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/setting-standards/recognising-learning-and-development-programmes/", "verified": true, "note": "Landing page; document deep link to confirm"}, {"match": "AAQA", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/setting-standards/recognising-learning-and-development-programmes/", "verified": true, "note": "Landing page; document deep link to confirm"}, {"match": "Regulations for Licensing", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/our-role-as-regulator/licensing-professional-engineering-institutions/", "verified": true, "note": "Licensing page; RfL deep link to confirm"}, {"match": "IPD Policy Statement", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/resources-and-guidance/professional-development/initial-professional-development-ipd/", "verified": true, "note": "IPD landing; Policy Statement PDF to confirm"}, {"match": "CPD Policy Statement", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/resources-and-guidance/professional-development/continuing-professional-development-cpd/", "verified": true, "note": "CPD landing; Policy Statement PDF to confirm"}, {"match": "recognised programmes database", "url": "https://www.engc.org.uk/education-and-skills/find-your-course/course-search/", "verified": true, "note": "Course search"}, {"match": "Welsh Language Act 1993", "url": "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1993/38/contents", "verified": false, "note": "legislation.gov.uk pattern; unfetched"}, {"match": "Code of Practice on Document Hierarchy and Associated Language", "url": null, "verified": false, "note": "Not public. Stub: would link to the CoP once published"}, {"match": "Guidance Note on CPD Sampling", "url": null, "verified": false, "note": "Partner Portal document; stub"}, {"match": "Guidance Note on Recognised Standards", "url": null, "verified": false, "note": "Superseded in this build by the GN overlay on V5-012 (gn-overlays.json); stub elsewhere"}, {"match": "A list of current agreements", "url": null, "verified": false, "note": "Partner Portal IAP page; public home to be decided (OP-15). Stub"}]}, "journey": {"note": "Seed only, generated from Table 1. M5: Table 1 is not the applicant-journey map. Ben will add model diagrams (MP's and the Institute of Water's, per M4) in journey-models/; follow those for form. Interim registration branches off after the UKU stage; appeals branch off after the decision.", "branches": [{"from": "Assessment of underpinning knowledge and understanding", "label": "Interim registration", "anchor": "interim-registration"}, {"from": "Registration decision", "label": "Appeal", "anchor": "appeals"}, {"from": "Professional Review: interview", "label": "Exemption under an international agreement", "anchor": "applicants-covered-by-international-agreements"}], "stages": [{"stage": "Initial assessment", "what": "Clerical check that the application is complete and the evidence is genuine", "who": "Licensee staff, under the committee responsible for registration", "gate": "Application complete and verified before any peer review", "clauses": ["V5-017", "V5-018", "V5-076"], "anchor": "initial-assessment"}, {"stage": "Assessment of underpinning knowledge and understanding", "what": "Determination that UKU meets the requirement, and any gap identified and addressed", "who": "Peers, drawing on verified evidence; supported by staff for clerical checks", "gate": "UKU met before final-stage registration. Interim registration is possible from this point", "clauses": ["UKU-001", "UKU-003", "UKU-004", "UKU-006", "UKU-007", "UKU-008", "UKU-009", "UKU-011", "UKU-012", "UKU-014", "UKU-016", "UKU-017", "UKU-018"], "anchor": "assessment-of-underpinning-knowledge-and-understanding"}, {"stage": "Professional Review: review of documentary evidence", "what": "First peer assessment of competence and commitment", "who": "Two peers", "gate": "Recommendation from the assessors of the documentary evidence before interview", "clauses": ["V5-019", "V5-020", "V5-022", "V5-077"], "anchor": "review-of-documentary-evidence"}, {"stage": "Professional Review: interview", "what": "Second peer assessment of competence and commitment", "who": "Two peers", "gate": "Both assessors recommend registration", "clauses": ["V5-022", "V5-024", "V5-078"], "anchor": "interview"}, {"stage": "Registration decision", "what": "The committee decides whether to register", "who": "Committee responsible for registration", "gate": "-", "clauses": ["V5-025", "V5-026"], "anchor": "registration-decision"}]}};
